WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.219 --> 00:00:16.810
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies
and germs.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:17.810
This is Tim Ferriss.

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:19.699
Welcome to another episode of The Tim Ferriss
Show.

00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:24.440
I'm going to keep my intro spiel as short
as possible because my guest today is someone

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.630
I want to ask many, many questions.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:31.159
Danny Meyer, you can find them on Twitter
@dhmeyer, M-E-Y-E-R, is the founder and chairman

00:00:31.159 --> 00:00:37.020
of Union Square Hospitality Group, USHG, which
comprises some of New York's most beloved

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:41.530
and acclaimed restaurants, including Gramercy
Tavern, the modern Maialino, and more.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:43.440
I have been to most of them.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.940
As a strong island native, I have made the
track several times.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:52.239
Danny and USHG also founded Shake Shack, which
you may have heard of, the modern day roadside

00:00:52.239 --> 00:00:55.510
burger restaurant, which became a public company
in 2015.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:59.010
Danny is the author of the New York Times
Bestseller, Setting the Table, which articulates

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:03.090
a set of signature business and life principles
that translate to a wide range of industries.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:04.790
We'll be digging into that.

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:09.240
He's the recipient of the 2017 Julia Child
Award and was named by Time Magazine as one

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.189
of 2015's 100 most influential people.

00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:17.530
Danny and USHG's Restaurants and individuals
together have won an unprecedented 28 James

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:22.310
Beard Awards, think of those as the Oscars
of food, including outstanding restaurateur

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:24.390
in 2005.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.790
Danny, so nice to finally connect live.

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:30.159
Danny Meyer: I know, it's about time.

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:32.189
It's great to see you and to be with you,
Tim.

00:01:32.189 --> 00:01:36.010
Tim Ferriss: I have so many pages in front
of me and so many questions.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:40.899
It's really just been a challenge, an embarrassment
of riches in terms of where to start, but

00:01:40.899 --> 00:01:47.049
I thought I would start with Maialino, so
little pig, little piglet.

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:49.180
What is the backstory on the name?

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:54.020
Danny Meyer: Back in, I think I was about
20 years old, I got to work for my dad, who

00:01:54.020 --> 00:02:00.299
was in the travel business, and he was selling
these group tours to airline employees and

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:01.299
their families.

00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:05.770
It was this crazy idea he had, that he had
this niche market, and he was going to aggregate

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:10.800
all of the kind of benefits and discounts
that you would get as an airline employee

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.319
and package them all together to create these
tours.

00:02:15.319 --> 00:02:20.410
So by the time my brother, sister, and I each
turned 20, we were each invited by my dad

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:26.769
to go work as a tour guide in the city of
our choice, and my sister had picked Denmark

00:02:26.769 --> 00:02:30.080
or Copenhagen, I picked Rome, my brother would
later pick Paris.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:37.960
So I'm in Rome, and this was one of the most
pivotal parts of my entire life experience

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:42.300
that would probably direct where I ended up
in my career.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:46.739
So I was the guy that would wake up every
single morning early, go pick up these cranky

00:02:46.739 --> 00:02:48.860
tourists.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:52.551
They were pilots, flight attendants, baggage
handlers, because keep in mind, they were

00:02:52.551 --> 00:02:54.069
all airline employees.

00:02:54.069 --> 00:03:00.000
I'd pick them up at the Rome airport, collect
all their luggage, get them on the tour bus.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.310
I'd get on the microphone as a 20-year-old,
tell them all about what the next three days

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:06.690
were going to be like.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:11.659
Here's how you've got to hold your purse so
you don't get it stolen by someone on a motorcycle.

00:03:11.659 --> 00:03:13.839
Here's what you should or should, blah, blah,
blah.

00:03:13.839 --> 00:03:21.799
So anyway, I was supposed to be taking these
tours to all the typical places like the cameo

00:03:21.799 --> 00:03:29.379
factories and the Vatican Museum, all the
stuff that was on their itinerary, but instead

00:03:29.379 --> 00:03:33.040
I was looking at this as an opportunity to
get an education in food.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.500
So I kept going to these trattorias.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:41.830
I found three different trattorias that would
actually pay me a 1,000 lira per head of every

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:43.480
guest I brought in.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:51.239
So I'd go get free food at these really good
restaurants, and I'd end up with 25,000 lira

00:03:51.239 --> 00:03:52.720
in my pocket afterwards.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:58.720
Well, so one of the restaurants, which was
called La Taverna da Giovanni, started calling

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:04.610
me Meyerino, Little Meyer, because they knew
it was my dad's company.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.969
I thought that was kind of cute.

00:04:06.969 --> 00:04:12.860
And somehow over the course of that summer,
without my inexperienced ear really picking

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:22.050
it up, Meyerino, Little Meyer, had somehow
changed to Maialino, which means little pig.

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:27.070
The reason they did that, and the joke was
on me, but every single time I went to this

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:32.570
restaurant, my favorite thing to order was
the Maialino, the roast suckling pig.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:40.260
So fast forward many, many years later, on
my 50th birthday, my wife Audrey, she knows

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:48.070
I hate surprises, so the party was not a surprise,
but what was a surprise to me is that she

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:57.030
had co-created a logo for my birthday, which
was Maialino, and she had my picture as a

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:04.370
little kid with a pig underneath it and made
these stickers for bottles of wine that everybody

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:05.449
was drinking that night.

00:05:05.449 --> 00:05:12.289
And it was such a damn good logo that when
it was time to open a new restaurant, I couldn't

00:05:12.289 --> 00:05:14.070
come up with a better name or logo.

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:17.580
So we ended up naming a restaurant Maialino
after this whole experience.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:21.770
You can call me Little Pig or Little Meyer,
I'll answer to either one.

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:28.590
Tim Ferriss: Now, in the course of doing homework
for this conversation, I spotted, and this

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:33.340
is true for I suppose everyone on some level,
but sliding door moments, you've had so many

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:38.050
different sliding door moments where your
life could have cut one way or was headed

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:44.210
one direction and then seemed to pull a right
or a left turn at 90 degrees.

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:50.600
But before I get to asking about one of those,
specifically your uncle and prepping for the

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:53.750
LSATs, you mentioned you hate surprises.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:57.000
Why do you hate surprises, or how do you think
about surprises?

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:02.060
Danny Meyer: I think underneath it, I'm a
little bit of a control freak, and I realize

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:09.949
that there's this great expression my grandfather
taught me many times, which is "Man plans,

00:06:09.949 --> 00:06:12.130
and God laughs."

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:17.509
So as much as you think that you're planning
for success or planning for the stuff that's

00:06:17.509 --> 00:06:21.539
going to work out, the world usually has another
idea for you.

00:06:21.539 --> 00:06:28.770
So on the other hand, look, if we're driving,
I like to be behind the wheel.

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:34.720
It's my wiring, so I don't mind, I never mind
not knowing where the story's going to end,

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:40.910
but I like to have some, at least it may be
a false sense, but I like to have some sense

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:45.140
that at least I had some say in the matter.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:49.130
I mean, I'll be flying on an airplane, and
it's not that I need to get in the cockpit

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:55.819
with the pilot, but for example, I'm the absolute
world's worst sleeper on airplanes.

00:06:55.819 --> 00:06:59.940
I've got the window open, it's almost as if
I'm afraid I'm going to miss one of the clouds

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:02.979
outside the window if I don't keep paying
attention.

00:07:02.979 --> 00:07:09.880
Tim Ferriss: I think I share some similar
programming, and it brings to mind for me

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:16.620
this metaphor that a novelist once shared
with me, which was writing a novel is like

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:20.540
driving a car cross country with the headlights
on.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:24.500
You can't see where you're going, but you
see enough in front of you that at least you

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.340
know you're headed in the right direction.

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:32.599
So coming back to what I alluded to a little
bit earlier, and I know that we're establishing,

00:07:32.599 --> 00:07:36.190
setting the table, as it were, for people
listening who may not have a whole lot of

00:07:36.190 --> 00:07:43.250
background, you studied political science,
if I'm getting my facts straight.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:45.780
Then at one point you were preparing for the
LSAT.

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:52.319
So you're thinking of pursuing law education,
perhaps becoming a lawyer, and you had a conversation

00:07:52.319 --> 00:07:53.360
with your uncle.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:55.030
Could you describe that conversation?

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:56.030
And then I have a few follow-ups.

00:07:56.030 --> 00:07:57.030
Danny Meyer: Sure, yeah.

00:07:57.030 --> 00:08:03.220
I mean, with a poli sci degree, and the reason
that I did study political science is as a

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:11.889
child of pretty much the 1970s, middle child
of three in the middlemost state in the country,

00:08:11.889 --> 00:08:13.509
Missouri.

00:08:13.509 --> 00:08:17.270
Republican dad, Democratic mom.

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:19.919
Every night at the dinner table there was
a "discussion" about politics.

00:08:19.919 --> 00:08:20.919
Tim Ferriss: Discussion.

00:08:20.919 --> 00:08:28.470
Danny Meyer: It could have been Watergate,
it could have been Vietnam.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:34.360
The dinner table was where our family, we
could agree on what we wanted to eat, and

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:36.279
that was kind of about it.

00:08:36.279 --> 00:08:38.880
The food was the comfort.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:44.010
I, as the middle child, I was the one who
wanted to make everybody feel good and bring

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:47.630
everybody together and keep the family together.

00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:55.510
On the other hand, as much as my dad and I
were best pals, played a lot of sports together,

00:08:55.510 --> 00:09:01.290
just we loved, we cooked together all the
time, it was the time I spent with my mom

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:08.380
hanging out watching the news every night
that I found most interesting.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:14.480
So I knew I loved what we used to call current
events, I just was taken by it.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:19.260
So after I graduated with my poli sci degree,
I was either going to be interested in going

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:23.470
into politics, because what else do you do
with that kind of degree?

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:27.880
Or maybe I was going to go into journalism,
because I just liked either writing about

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:31.270
or impacting the events of the day.

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:36.890
So after I spent a few years being a salesman
just to make some money selling electronic

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:41.100
tags to stop shoplifters of all things, I
said, "You know what?

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:43.450
That's not what you want to do the rest of
your life."

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:48.610
It's been good, you made a bunch of commissions,
and I actually invested all those commissions

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:52.470
in that company stock, which was a good thing.

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:54.370
But I said, "You've got to do something right
now."

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:55.990
So I did take the LSATs.

00:09:55.990 --> 00:10:01.560
I decided a law degree instead of a journalism
degree.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:06.740
First of all, I think anyone who knows me
knows I would've made the world's worst lawyer

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:11.390
because I don't wake up every morning saying,
"I'm looking for a fight.

00:10:11.390 --> 00:10:13.560
I'm looking to prosecute."

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:18.560
It's kind of the opposite, I think hospitality
is the opposite, which is, "How do we bring

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:19.870
people together?"

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:26.320
But it was on the eve of taking my LSATs,
I had taken the Kaplan course, preparing for

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:28.440
it and everything, hated every minute of it.

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:33.970
The night before going to take this test was
a Friday night when I was out to dinner with

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:39.940
my aunt and uncle and my grandmother in New
York City at an Italian restaurant I still

00:10:39.940 --> 00:10:42.889
go to called Elio's.

00:10:42.889 --> 00:10:49.930
I was in a foul, just awful mood because first
of all, I didn't want to take the test, didn't

00:10:49.930 --> 00:10:57.140
want to be a lawyer, and my table mates were
all having a great time eating good pasta,

00:10:57.140 --> 00:10:59.540
drinking lots of wine, and I couldn't do it.

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:06.620
So at a certain point my uncle, my Uncle Richard,
turns to me and he says, "What the hell is

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:08.360
bothering you anyway?"

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:11.720
And I said, "Well, I've got to take my LSAT
tomorrow morning."

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:13.579
And he said, "Oh, of course you do.

00:11:13.579 --> 00:11:15.070
You want to be a lawyer, don't you?"

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:18.310
And I said, "Actually, no."

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:24.269
And basically he wanted to throw his pasta
spoon at me at that moment, and he asked me

00:11:24.269 --> 00:11:31.180
what was, to this day, the most impactful
question of my whole life, which was one I

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:32.180
was not expecting.

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:37.120
He said, "Do you have any idea how long you're
going to be dead anyway?"

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:40.570
And I said, "No, I hadn't really thought about
that.

00:11:40.570 --> 00:11:41.570
Why?"

00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:44.360
And he said, "I don't know either, but I'll
tell you one thing.

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:47.160
You're going to be dead a hell of a lot longer
than you're going to be alive.

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:53.800
Why in the world would you do something that
you have no passion around?"

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:59.829
And I stopped and I said, "Because I guess
I don't know what else I could do."

00:11:59.829 --> 00:12:02.720
And within a second, he said, "You've got
to be kidding me.

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:08.380
All I've heard you talk about your entire
life is food and restaurants."

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:14.120
And I said, "So what, am I supposed to eat
in restaurants the rest of my life?"

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:18.550
It was so obvious, and yet I could not see
this.

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:19.600
And he said, "No, you fool.

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:21.430
You should go open a restaurant."

00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:27.870
It had never dawned on me that that was a
valid thing to do, because that's not what

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:29.949
you ever heard about in college back then.

00:12:29.949 --> 00:12:32.930
You didn't hear about going to open a restaurant.

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:38.450
Now, I'm really, really glad that all these
years later being in the food business has

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:45.040
become a validated entrepreneurial career
choice for people within education, but it

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:50.140
wasn't back then, and it's still — well
here, I'll cut to the chase.

00:12:50.140 --> 00:12:51.899
I did take the LSAT the next morning.

00:12:51.899 --> 00:12:57.370
I had already paid for it, for God's sakes,
never applied to one school.

00:12:57.370 --> 00:13:03.860
But what I did do is the following Monday
morning, I connected with one of my best buddies

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:08.860
from Trinity College who I used to go out
to eat with all the time and was a fraternity

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:11.000
brother, and I said, "I've got this idea.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:12.770
I'm going to open a restaurant.

00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:15.000
You be the money guy, I'll be the food guy.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:16.490
What do you think?"

00:13:16.490 --> 00:13:21.810
And he was in a bank training program at that
point, and he said, "Okay, I'll do it."

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:27.880
So we enrolled in the New York Restaurant
School, which was all you had to do was pay

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:31.829
your 150 bucks and you got in.

00:13:31.829 --> 00:13:37.750
We took a restaurant management class, and
he promptly dropped out after two sessions

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:42.690
because he made the mistake of telling his
parents that he was thinking about leaving

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:47.460
banking to go into the restaurant business.

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:52.160
Bottom line is he felt so bad about leaving
me all alone that he said, "Look, our bank

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:54.670
has one restaurant client."

00:13:54.670 --> 00:13:58.710
Which was a big deal because mostly banks
would run the other way if they heard the

00:13:58.710 --> 00:14:00.110
word restaurant back then.

00:14:00.110 --> 00:14:04.070
And he said, "I'll be glad to see if I can
get you an interview with that.

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:05.780
Would you like that?"

00:14:05.780 --> 00:14:07.500
So I got the interview.

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:13.420
The interview pretty much consisted of the
owner sitting midway down his bar.

00:14:13.420 --> 00:14:20.240
I'm at the front door, he waves me down the
bar, tells me to stop and stand right in front

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:21.240
of him.

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:25.670
He looks me up and down from my wallabies
up to my Brooks Brothers shirt.

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:29.620
He goes, "You'll do."

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:30.829
That was my first job interview.

00:14:30.829 --> 00:14:39.020
I got the job and I was assistant lunch manager,
which meant nothing, which meant I was getting

00:14:39.020 --> 00:14:45.990
250 bucks a week to answer reservation lines
and set up the reservation book for lunch

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:48.639
and be on the front door.

00:14:48.639 --> 00:14:49.639
Here's the good news.

00:14:49.639 --> 00:14:55.390
Out of that deal, I've figured out in seven
months that I love this business.

00:14:55.390 --> 00:14:59.770
I met the woman who would become my wife.

00:14:59.770 --> 00:15:03.870
She was a waitress, an actress.

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:08.550
She actually left on day two to go get an
acting job and I couldn't stop thinking about

00:15:08.550 --> 00:15:11.660
having just seen her for one second.

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:17.830
I met the neighborhood in which Union Square
Cafe, and Gramercy Tavern, and so many of

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:24.180
our restaurants have been, and I met my career.

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:31.690
That was a major, major pivot point, but it
just shows you so many things in life are

00:15:31.690 --> 00:15:32.690
those moments.

00:15:32.690 --> 00:15:39.329
I mean, the fact that none of this ever would've
happened if I had not gone to Trinity College,

00:15:39.329 --> 00:15:45.100
for example, because that's where I met my
friend, and I was, oh for three applying.

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:50.600
I was such a screw up in high school, I applied
to only three colleges.

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:58.080
I was rejected from two and waitlisted at
Trinity, and I had to get down on my hands

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:04.490
and knees and write the best letter of my
life to get off the wait list to Trinity.

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:09.569
So first of all, that had to happen, and the
only reason that Trinity happened is that

00:16:09.569 --> 00:16:16.880
when my dad's business was in Rome, his travel
business, he met Trinity College's lawyer

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:22.040
at dinner one night, and the lawyer said,
"Hey, your son should consider Trinity College."

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:24.220
Which none of us had ever heard of.

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:30.760
So all that stuff, and then by the way, this
friend of mine who introduced me to his restaurant

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:37.459
client, the only reason we ever met is on
the first night that I was at Trinity College,

00:16:37.459 --> 00:16:43.029
there was a pickup softball game and there
weren't enough baseball gloves to go around,

00:16:43.029 --> 00:16:47.670
and so I lent my glove and I went up to this
guy and accused him of stealing my baseball

00:16:47.670 --> 00:16:50.759
glove, and somehow we became friends after
that.

00:16:50.759 --> 00:16:56.010
So just all these moments that didn't have
to happen and that it happens in life every

00:16:56.010 --> 00:17:02.240
day, and you've just got to pay attention
and just be grateful if you're fortunate enough

00:17:02.240 --> 00:17:07.650
to have a good choice based on stuff that
never should have happened in the first place.

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:09.520
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:10.660
It's so wild.

00:17:10.660 --> 00:17:16.299
So I have a number of follow up questions,
which will dig a bit more into a few of these

00:17:16.299 --> 00:17:18.150
bits and pieces.

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:24.770
So the first is, and this will lead to a question
about not applying to law school.

00:17:24.770 --> 00:17:30.620
When you were working in Rome and you would,
say, have a group of four or five people,

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:35.760
you picked the crankiest and they become your,
not mark, but your objective in the sense

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:43.390
that you're going to turn them into a happy
raving fan by the end.

00:17:43.390 --> 00:17:49.690
What were the keys or techniques, what did
you learn over time as most reliable for doing

00:17:49.690 --> 00:17:50.790
that for that person?

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:57.980
Danny Meyer: I was probably in five different
schools growing up in St. Louis.

00:17:57.980 --> 00:18:02.580
So there was a nursery school, then there
was a public school, then we moved, so there

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:04.000
was another public school.

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:08.950
Then I went to an all boys school because
I wanted to play football on their team.

00:18:08.950 --> 00:18:13.419
Then 10th grade came and I wanted to go to
school with girls, so I went to a co-ed school

00:18:13.419 --> 00:18:14.770
after that.

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:16.860
So that was five schools.

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:22.350
Then by the time I was 18, now I'm going to
college, so now I'm in six schools by the

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:23.780
time I'm 18.

00:18:23.780 --> 00:18:27.909
You've got to learn a lot of social cues along
the way.

00:18:27.909 --> 00:18:35.750
I just found that I was really good at kind
of understanding what made people tick and

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:41.220
a really good observer of body language, moods,
et cetera.

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:47.480
And it's not that I didn't have a sense of
myself, where I wasn't like Zelig or a chameleon

00:18:47.480 --> 00:18:52.010
changing who I was, but I knew what people
needed.

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:57.080
Kind of a blessing to have found a career
where that is a really useful thing to be

00:18:57.080 --> 00:18:58.080
able to do.

00:18:58.080 --> 00:19:03.380
And as I said, even before going into the
restaurant business for three years, I was

00:19:03.380 --> 00:19:11.110
the top salesman in this company as a young
20-year-old selling against hardened salesmen

00:19:11.110 --> 00:19:14.669
from around the country.

00:19:14.669 --> 00:19:15.850
I loved it.

00:19:15.850 --> 00:19:21.050
I loved getting into my little blue rabbit
and traveling to the worst neighborhoods in

00:19:21.050 --> 00:19:26.080
New York where there was the worst amount
of shoplifting, and meeting these people,

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:35.110
these shopkeepers who owned drug stores, bookstores,
clothing stores, fur stores, supermarkets,

00:19:35.110 --> 00:19:40.950
and learning to speak their language and making
the sale.

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:42.080
I just loved it.

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:47.320
So I just think that I'm cut out to do this,
and the fact that I happen to love food and

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:50.549
wine as much as I do has certainly helped
a lot.

00:19:50.549 --> 00:20:01.429
Tim Ferriss: So I would agree, for sure, that
you are well cut to excel in these various

00:20:01.429 --> 00:20:06.659
areas, and there have been various points
where you've had to, for lack of a better

00:20:06.659 --> 00:20:13.039
term, maybe operationalize or externalize
yourself so that you could, say, expand in

00:20:13.039 --> 00:20:14.039
the restaurant business.

00:20:14.039 --> 00:20:15.669
So I'm curious, I mean, you mentioned making
ends meet.

00:20:15.669 --> 00:20:19.900
I was going to say, that was Checkpoint, am
I getting that right, or was it Checkpoint

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:20.900
Systems?

00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:21.900
Danny Meyer: Yeah, Checkpoint Systems.

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:22.900
Tim Ferriss: Checkpoint Systems.

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:27.309
If you had to try to break down what you did,
let's just say in sales at Checkpoint Systems

00:20:27.309 --> 00:20:31.890
that made you so successful so that you could
teach somebody else, does anything come to

00:20:31.890 --> 00:20:38.750
mind that you think somebody else could emulate
or principles perhaps they could attempt to

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:41.220
implement if they were in that same job?

00:20:41.220 --> 00:20:47.250
Danny Meyer: Well, the first thing is, keep
in mind this was before we had the internet,

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:51.870
but I would still do a lot of research, and
I would still learn as much as I could possibly

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:57.630
learn, not only about the business that I
was trying to sell, but the person who I'd

00:20:57.630 --> 00:21:00.220
be meeting with.

00:21:00.220 --> 00:21:06.270
What I came to learn very, very quickly in
the New York retail world, and keep in mind,

00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:11.480
I was primarily selling to retailers because
that's who had the shoplifting issues, is

00:21:11.480 --> 00:21:16.500
that many, many, many of them were either
related to each other.

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:18.850
There was a huge Syrian Jew population.

00:21:18.850 --> 00:21:24.059
I'm not just saying that was not the only
population, but I started to develop a sense

00:21:24.059 --> 00:21:31.480
for the family trees of either real families
or relationships, and I got to know who knew

00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:37.110
who, and I would take that as far as I could
possibly take it.

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:43.281
So I learned early on something that has been
something that I teach our teams, even in

00:21:43.281 --> 00:21:46.549
the restaurant business, we call it ABCD so
you can ABCD.

00:21:46.549 --> 00:21:49.780
Always be collecting dots so you can always
be connecting dots.

00:21:49.780 --> 00:21:55.429
I learned early on that people will take exactly
as much interest in you as they believe you're

00:21:55.429 --> 00:21:59.020
taking in them, no more and no less.

00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:04.010
So look, I don't want to give away all my
trade secrets here, Tim, but I've listened

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:10.850
to your show for a long time, but I listened
to a couple more segments much more recently

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:16.150
because I really want to understand more about
what makes you tick.

00:22:16.150 --> 00:22:18.230
I don't look at it as gaming the system.

00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:22.750
I think it's genuinely being curious and being
interested.

00:22:22.750 --> 00:22:25.260
So if you're looking for one tip, it's curiosity.

00:22:25.260 --> 00:22:27.059
And I should throw in one other thing.

00:22:27.059 --> 00:22:29.130
The real trick was that I would pick my, since
I was largely cold calling people or often

00:22:29.130 --> 00:22:34.270
cold calling people, I would actually make
my own schedule for where I was going to go

00:22:34.270 --> 00:22:40.909
that day based on a restaurant that I wanted
to try in that particular borough or neighborhood

00:22:40.909 --> 00:22:42.279
of New York.

00:22:42.279 --> 00:22:48.110
I would organize my day around my lunch, or
sometimes I'd organize it.

00:22:48.110 --> 00:22:54.870
I even organize it around some guy who would
bring in lobsters in Brooklyn at exactly 2:00

00:22:54.870 --> 00:22:58.220
in the afternoon, and I'd go get his lobsters
and bring them home to cook.

00:22:58.220 --> 00:23:03.310
But it's in a weird way, I was developing
two careers at the same time without even

00:23:03.310 --> 00:23:04.310
thinking about it.

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:10.309
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, you were collecting dots
in a few different areas at the same time,

00:23:10.309 --> 00:23:16.789
and also giving yourself, it seems like, small
rewards, which would probably give you more

00:23:16.789 --> 00:23:21.330
endurance in doing what you were doing in
terms of gathering or organizing your schedule

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:22.830
around those lunch spots.

00:23:22.830 --> 00:23:31.299
Tim Ferriss: How did you navigate the conversation,
and how did your parents respond to the conversation

00:23:31.299 --> 00:23:33.809
related to not applying to law school?

00:23:33.809 --> 00:23:37.760
Danny Meyer: Well, it was easy at first because
I didn't tell them.

00:23:37.760 --> 00:23:43.659
I kind of sidetracked and side stepped.

00:23:43.659 --> 00:23:51.500
I finally one day got the courage to say that
I wanted to be a chef.

00:23:51.500 --> 00:23:53.080
They knew I loved to cook.

00:23:53.080 --> 00:23:56.510
Now you may say, well, how would you get the
courage to say you wanted to be a chef but

00:23:56.510 --> 00:23:58.059
not to be a restaurateur?

00:23:58.059 --> 00:24:02.070
Well, in fact, I thought I did want to be
a chef.

00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:09.130
I had seen at that point, there were a number
of really well educated people who had gone

00:24:09.130 --> 00:24:15.140
into the culinary profession, and chefs were
starting to become kind of well known.

00:24:15.140 --> 00:24:20.700
Keep in mind, this was way before the Food
Network, but you know, you had people like

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:28.510
Alice Waters who had gone to Berkeley, you
had Jeremiah Tower who had gone to Harvard,

00:24:28.510 --> 00:24:34.740
Joyce Goldstein on the West Coast, Mark Miller,
on and on and on.

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:39.460
There's probably like 15 of them, and Wolfgang
Puck had become a household name.

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:43.490
Paul Prudhomme in New Orleans had become a
household name.

00:24:43.490 --> 00:24:47.850
So I finally gathered the courage to say,
"I think I want to be a chef."

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:54.600
And by the way, in these days, I spent almost
all of my time walking around the city looking

00:24:54.600 --> 00:24:56.260
at menus on restaurants.

00:24:56.260 --> 00:24:58.000
I memorized every menu.

00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:02.289
I ate at many of the restaurants.

00:25:02.289 --> 00:25:06.130
I would go back to Italy many, many times,
not just when I was a tour guide working for

00:25:06.130 --> 00:25:15.600
my dad, but until I was 21 years old, I could
travel anywhere Pan Am flew for $44 round

00:25:15.600 --> 00:25:18.300
trip, thanks to my dad's travel business.

00:25:18.300 --> 00:25:23.360
And so I was constantly going to restaurants
and learning and learning and learning.

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:27.130
And, anyway, so I finally said, "I'm going
to be a chef."

00:25:27.130 --> 00:25:33.670
And so my dad was kind of open to it, my mom
maybe a little bit less so, but I went for

00:25:33.670 --> 00:25:34.670
it.

00:25:34.670 --> 00:25:44.230
My dad actually connected me with two of the
Relais &amp; Châteaux colleagues of his in Bordeaux.

00:25:44.230 --> 00:25:51.540
He said, "Look, I know you want to go to Italy,
but in Italy, they basically cook food with

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:55.370
three ingredients, and that's the genius of
Italian cooking.

00:25:55.370 --> 00:25:58.370
But if you really, really want to learn to
cook, you've got to go to France.

00:25:58.370 --> 00:26:00.380
So I said, "Well, let me do both."

00:26:00.380 --> 00:26:05.960
So I spent time in Rome, Bologna, Sardinia,
Milan.

00:26:05.960 --> 00:26:14.730
And then my dad did connect me with a restaurateur
in Bordeaux named [foreign language].

00:26:14.730 --> 00:26:17.710
And he had two restaurants in Bordeaux.

00:26:17.710 --> 00:26:24.700
One was called La Réserve, and the other
was was called Dubern, D-U-B-E-R-N.

00:26:24.700 --> 00:26:28.880
The day before I got to La Réserve, they
had lost their second Michelin star.

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:35.940
Now chefs have actually gone off the deep
end for less because Michelin stars were everything

00:26:35.940 --> 00:26:37.860
for the French.

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:41.800
And so I get there and everybody in the kitchen
is completely dejected.

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:46.770
And I'm this guy who doesn't know anything
about cooking and who is he and how did he

00:26:46.770 --> 00:26:53.350
get in our kitchen and why are they letting
him live with the chef?

00:26:53.350 --> 00:26:59.740
And the good news was that after a couple
days, a bunch of people in that kitchen left

00:26:59.740 --> 00:27:02.770
because they did not want their resume to
say one star.

00:27:02.770 --> 00:27:03.770
Tim Ferriss: To be tarnished.

00:27:03.770 --> 00:27:07.690
Danny Meyer: So they figured if they left
with two stars on the resume, they'd be fine.

00:27:07.690 --> 00:27:12.770
And so all of a sudden, I started to get some
opportunities, some really big opportunities,

00:27:12.770 --> 00:27:19.890
like chopping shallots and opening oysters
and pulling feathers out of pigeons to prepare

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:21.730
them.

00:27:21.730 --> 00:27:22.730
And here was the big one.

00:27:22.730 --> 00:27:28.519
I got to cook family meal, which that's a
big deal to get to cook for all the other

00:27:28.519 --> 00:27:29.519
cooks.

00:27:29.519 --> 00:27:32.450
And I was doing some of my family's favorite
recipes.

00:27:32.450 --> 00:27:34.120
I'll never forget when I made barbecued ribs.

00:27:34.120 --> 00:27:40.450
[foreign language 00:34:21] They didn't even
know how to eat these things.

00:27:40.450 --> 00:27:42.120
It was just a great, great experience.

00:27:42.120 --> 00:27:48.510
They took me to oyster beds, they took me
to chateaus in Bordeaux.

00:27:48.510 --> 00:27:52.610
They took me to places where they made sausage.

00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:58.310
They took me on one of my favorite days of
my life, a day that the restaurant was closed

00:27:58.310 --> 00:28:03.070
to go hunting for wild pigeons called [inaudible].

00:28:03.070 --> 00:28:04.420
Just a great life experience.

00:28:04.420 --> 00:28:09.880
Tim Ferriss: I thought that it might be helpful
to people listening, and I'd certainly love

00:28:09.880 --> 00:28:15.159
to hear your thoughts on, for lack of a better
way to put it, free work.

00:28:15.159 --> 00:28:21.230
So in the food world, you have people who
will stage, and maybe you could describe what

00:28:21.230 --> 00:28:22.230
that means.

00:28:22.230 --> 00:28:28.600
But my understanding, based on our mutual
friend Bill Gurley's speech at UT, I think

00:28:28.600 --> 00:28:31.870
it's called "Runnin' Down a Dream," might
have a slightly different title to it, but

00:28:31.870 --> 00:28:33.090
I recommend everybody see it.

00:28:33.090 --> 00:28:40.159
There's a 10-minute segment on your arc, and
he presents it really well.

00:28:40.159 --> 00:28:47.200
And he talks about how you went from star
salesman at Checkpoint Systems and then, over

00:28:47.200 --> 00:28:51.320
time, got to zero and then kind of went upside
down because I think some of these restaurants

00:28:51.320 --> 00:28:56.120
asked you to pay them for the privilege of
working there.

00:28:56.120 --> 00:29:00.760
Could you just speak to the logic or thinking
behind that?

00:29:00.760 --> 00:29:03.810
Maybe it's as simple as, "Hey, I didn't have
a choice and I really wanted to do this,"

00:29:03.810 --> 00:29:10.820
but it seems to me that a lot of younger generations
now have the impulse to say, "I want to be

00:29:10.820 --> 00:29:13.050
paid what I'm worth."

00:29:13.050 --> 00:29:19.380
And I see a lot of wisdom and value in the
secret weapon that a lot of young people have

00:29:19.380 --> 00:29:23.510
when they don't have a lot of money, they
have a lot of time, which is working for free

00:29:23.510 --> 00:29:25.360
and learning a lot.

00:29:25.360 --> 00:29:28.899
Would you be able to share any thoughts that
you have on that?

00:29:28.899 --> 00:29:34.529
Danny Meyer: In our industry, there's a sense
that you really, if you want to dig your roots

00:29:34.529 --> 00:29:37.790
and build a career, you should learn from
the best.

00:29:37.790 --> 00:29:40.760
And how do you get your foot in the door with
the best?

00:29:40.760 --> 00:29:44.620
So you were willing to work for nothing.

00:29:44.620 --> 00:29:50.190
The only time I ever had to pay anybody was
this cooking teacher in Milan who told me

00:29:50.190 --> 00:29:57.000
that she was the Julia Child of Italy, which
she certainly was not the Julia Child of Italy.

00:29:57.000 --> 00:29:58.670
I paid her my money and moved on.

00:29:58.670 --> 00:30:02.580
But I did many, many stages where I was not
paid anything.

00:30:02.580 --> 00:30:09.730
And as I told you, the one that I did in New
York City, I was paid 250 bucks a week, which

00:30:09.730 --> 00:30:11.190
kind of laughable.

00:30:11.190 --> 00:30:14.580
That's what a waiter makes in half of a night
in New York City these days.

00:30:14.580 --> 00:30:22.160
So, look, I just think that if I've learned
one thing, it took me 10 years to open a second

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:29.890
restaurant and today we'll probably open two
Shake Shacks somewhere in the world.

00:30:29.890 --> 00:30:34.820
If I've learned any one thing is that there's
a benefit to being a little bit patient and

00:30:34.820 --> 00:30:37.020
try to grow where you're planted.

00:30:37.020 --> 00:30:45.289
And I think that as life is accelerated for
so many reasons, technology being primary

00:30:45.289 --> 00:30:51.139
amongst those, I think that people feel like
there has to be a beginning, middle, and an

00:30:51.139 --> 00:30:55.720
end that all gets resolved within a 30-minute
sitcom.

00:30:55.720 --> 00:30:56.950
And that's just not how life works.

00:30:56.950 --> 00:31:03.740
And I think that if you can do time, paid
or not, but really learn what it is you're

00:31:03.740 --> 00:31:09.860
trying to do and not scratch the surface and
move on too quickly, it really pays big dividends.

00:31:09.860 --> 00:31:14.460
Now, there were reasons it took me 10 years
to open a second restaurant.

00:31:14.460 --> 00:31:17.419
Primary one was I didn't want to go bankrupt.

00:31:17.419 --> 00:31:22.900
And since I had seen my dad go through two
different bankruptcies, one in my teens and

00:31:22.900 --> 00:31:28.250
one when I was about 20-something, I forget
how old I was.

00:31:28.250 --> 00:31:31.399
And I always a associated his bankruptcies
with expansion.

00:31:31.399 --> 00:31:35.780
And I assumed that that was the business thing
he wanted to avoid was expansion.

00:31:35.780 --> 00:31:38.440
And I didn't want to end up like my dad.

00:31:38.440 --> 00:31:42.549
I just said, "There's no way I'm ever going
to open a second restaurant because I don't

00:31:42.549 --> 00:31:44.120
want that to happen."

00:31:44.120 --> 00:31:51.600
And the silver lining in that, even though
eventually after my dad died and I got some

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:56.039
therapy and the first thing I learned was,
"Hey, guess what, you're not your dad.

00:31:56.039 --> 00:32:00.600
And number two, there's a whole lot of businesses
that have expanded and that wasn't the reason

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:01.600
they went bankrupt.

00:32:01.600 --> 00:32:04.960
In fact, they didn't go bankrupt."

00:32:04.960 --> 00:32:09.299
But the silver lining was I really learned
my business.

00:32:09.299 --> 00:32:10.299
I really learned it.

00:32:10.299 --> 00:32:16.279
That was probably the reason it took us five
years to open a second Shake Shack.

00:32:16.279 --> 00:32:19.970
I didn't want to expand too quickly and I
did not want anything bad to happen.

00:32:19.970 --> 00:32:20.970
But guess what?

00:32:20.970 --> 00:32:21.970
We've learned our business.

00:32:21.970 --> 00:32:25.190
Tim Ferriss: This might seem like a naive
question, but what does learning the business

00:32:25.190 --> 00:32:26.850
look like?

00:32:26.850 --> 00:32:30.420
Because there's experience and then there's
developing expertise.

00:32:30.420 --> 00:32:35.720
Some people can repeat the same mistakes every
year for 20 years straight, and then there's

00:32:35.720 --> 00:32:36.720
learning a lot.

00:32:36.720 --> 00:32:37.720
And those are two different —
Danny Meyer: Yeah.

00:32:37.720 --> 00:32:39.820
Every business is made up of five stakeholders.

00:32:39.820 --> 00:32:45.429
And so when I say learning the business, it's
learning as much as you possibly can about

00:32:45.429 --> 00:32:50.279
how to motivate all five of your stakeholders
to root for your success.

00:32:50.279 --> 00:32:51.620
That's what you want.

00:32:51.620 --> 00:32:56.070
And so the best way to motivate all five of
those stakeholders to root for your success

00:32:56.070 --> 00:32:59.150
is to make sure that they believe you're on
their side first.

00:32:59.150 --> 00:33:02.240
So it was learning who's our staff?

00:33:02.240 --> 00:33:04.559
That was our first stakeholder.

00:33:04.559 --> 00:33:05.559
Who are they?

00:33:05.559 --> 00:33:08.200
Who are the best staff we could possibly have?

00:33:08.200 --> 00:33:09.880
Who are our guests?

00:33:09.880 --> 00:33:13.320
And I mean really learning about them.

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:22.350
And to this day, to this very day, I end every
single night reading all the reservation reports

00:33:22.350 --> 00:33:27.919
for all of our restaurants so I know who's
dining in our restaurants tomorrow.

00:33:27.919 --> 00:33:35.630
And then if I stay up really late, I read
all the after service reports or I'll do that

00:33:35.630 --> 00:33:39.019
first thing in the morning to find out and
how did all those experiences go?

00:33:39.019 --> 00:33:40.019
And I weigh in.

00:33:40.019 --> 00:33:45.019
And to this day I'm connecting dots because
I care about who our guests are.

00:33:45.019 --> 00:33:46.370
This one knows that one.

00:33:46.370 --> 00:33:47.770
Seat them near each other.

00:33:47.770 --> 00:33:49.810
This one has just published a book.

00:33:49.810 --> 00:33:54.519
Make sure to go buy that book and have it
on the maitre d' stand so they can sign it.

00:33:54.519 --> 00:33:59.620
I care about that stuff as much as I've ever
cared about it.

00:33:59.620 --> 00:34:04.390
But it also means getting to know the community
in which you do business because why should

00:34:04.390 --> 00:34:08.849
your community root for your success if you're
not investing in your community?

00:34:08.849 --> 00:34:11.379
Same thing goes true of our suppliers.

00:34:11.379 --> 00:34:14.050
Get to know your suppliers.

00:34:14.050 --> 00:34:15.260
You want the best product.

00:34:15.260 --> 00:34:18.330
You can't have the best business if your raw
products are no good.

00:34:18.330 --> 00:34:21.040
I learned that lesson, by the way, from my
grandmother.

00:34:21.040 --> 00:34:23.810
Very, very important.

00:34:23.810 --> 00:34:26.380
I loved the cooking in her home.

00:34:26.380 --> 00:34:32.750
And I'll never forget when I asked for her
tomato sauce recipe and she said, "I'll give

00:34:32.750 --> 00:34:35.409
you the recipe, but I'll tell you one thing
right now.

00:34:35.409 --> 00:34:40.909
It's never going to be any better than the
worst ingredients you put into it.

00:34:40.909 --> 00:34:45.149
And number two, those ingredients themselves
won't be any better than how well you treat

00:34:45.149 --> 00:34:46.870
them after you buy them.

00:34:46.870 --> 00:34:53.040
So you'd better get the best tomatoes, which
in New York, York is a two-month season."

00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:56.230
It's August and September.

00:34:56.230 --> 00:35:00.970
And even if you get the best tomatoes, you'd
better not throw them in the back corner of

00:35:00.970 --> 00:35:05.579
the walk-in refrigerator and bruise them so
that they lose their sugar.

00:35:05.579 --> 00:35:06.579
Treat them the right way.

00:35:06.579 --> 00:35:07.579
Well, guess what?

00:35:07.579 --> 00:35:09.740
Same thing goes for people.

00:35:09.740 --> 00:35:14.970
You can have the best recipe in the world
for how you hire people, but you'd better

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:16.410
pick the best tomatoes.

00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:20.630
You'd better treat them the right way if you
want the best sauce.

00:35:20.630 --> 00:35:23.310
So I've just learned so many lessons.

00:35:23.310 --> 00:35:27.609
It's amazing how many life lessons actually
apply to learning your business.

00:35:27.609 --> 00:35:30.390
Another great one that my grandmother taught
me.

00:35:30.390 --> 00:35:38.480
When I was a little kid growing up in St.
Louis, my favorite moment was March — March

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:43.670
and April — because spring came kind of
early to St. Louis.

00:35:43.670 --> 00:35:46.640
And that's when my grandmother would plant
her flower garden.

00:35:46.640 --> 00:35:48.780
She had a amazing green thumb.

00:35:48.780 --> 00:35:54.359
Well, keep in mind, she lived in an urban
apartment building, which was a skyscraper

00:35:54.359 --> 00:36:01.100
in St. Louis at six stories tall and her apartment
building gave her a plot of land in the parking

00:36:01.100 --> 00:36:09.500
lot that was probably 20 feet long by about
four feet wide.

00:36:09.500 --> 00:36:13.030
And she called it Carbon Monoxide Gardens.

00:36:13.030 --> 00:36:20.510
And so every spring she would invite me to
come help her plant her garden for the summer.

00:36:20.510 --> 00:36:25.810
And this would always be probably late March,
something like that.

00:36:25.810 --> 00:36:28.950
And this probably started when I was six years
old or something like that.

00:36:28.950 --> 00:36:35.720
And she gave me my gardening gloves and she
taught me early on to figure out which were

00:36:35.720 --> 00:36:36.920
the weeds.

00:36:36.920 --> 00:36:41.500
And kind of like that scene from Tom Sawyer
and Huckleberry Finn, she was basically having

00:36:41.500 --> 00:36:44.700
me do all of her free labor weeding the garden.

00:36:44.700 --> 00:36:47.119
So I learned to pick all the weeds.

00:36:47.119 --> 00:36:54.890
So now, by the time I'm about nine, I go down
there for my annual spring visit, and now

00:36:54.890 --> 00:37:00.270
it's probably April or May because I would
do this as the garden would keep growing.

00:37:00.270 --> 00:37:04.920
And she said, "Now I'm going to teach you
the real secret to how you have a great garden."

00:37:04.920 --> 00:37:09.980
And so I go for the weeds dutifully, and she
said, "Nope."

00:37:09.980 --> 00:37:17.329
She takes my hand, gently pulls it off the
weeds, and she hands me a watering bucket

00:37:17.329 --> 00:37:18.660
with something in it.

00:37:18.660 --> 00:37:19.839
I have no idea what was in it.

00:37:19.839 --> 00:37:23.630
And she says, "I'm going to teach you how
to water the flowers, because if you really

00:37:23.630 --> 00:37:28.319
want to get rid of the weeds, the best thing
you need to do is water the flowers because

00:37:28.319 --> 00:37:34.850
the flowers will provide a canopy that will
actually prevent the weeds from getting the

00:37:34.850 --> 00:37:38.130
sunlight that they actually need."

00:37:38.130 --> 00:37:46.480
So, voila, best business lesson ever because
I spent the first 10 years of my career almost

00:37:46.480 --> 00:37:55.130
exclusively focused on trying to motivate
problem employees to be better, like the weeds.

00:37:55.130 --> 00:37:59.520
And what I learned was that our employees
are like sunflowers.

00:37:59.520 --> 00:38:01.430
They will turn wherever the sun is.

00:38:01.430 --> 00:38:06.220
And if I'm spending all of my attention on
the weeds, I'm actually pulling the gravitational

00:38:06.220 --> 00:38:08.750
force that way.

00:38:08.750 --> 00:38:10.930
And my grandmother's lesson was right.

00:38:10.930 --> 00:38:15.940
If I water the flowers and spend more time
with the people who maybe I've taken for granted

00:38:15.940 --> 00:38:20.200
because they're doing such a great job, they
actually crowd out the weeds and the weeds

00:38:20.200 --> 00:38:21.819
take care of themselves.

00:38:21.819 --> 00:38:27.440
Tim Ferriss: I'm definitely going to ask you
more questions about hiring with some hypothetical

00:38:27.440 --> 00:38:31.359
situations, but before I move on, the five
stakeholders.

00:38:31.359 --> 00:38:32.359
Did you list them?

00:38:32.359 --> 00:38:37.910
And I think it was employees, guests, community,
suppliers, investors in roughly the order

00:38:37.910 --> 00:38:41.790
that you would, or did, weigh them in your
restaurants?

00:38:41.790 --> 00:38:43.980
Danny Meyer: Exactly.

00:38:43.980 --> 00:38:44.980
Exactly that order.

00:38:44.980 --> 00:38:46.920
It took a long time to figure that out.

00:38:46.920 --> 00:38:49.730
Every business has those same stakeholders.

00:38:49.730 --> 00:38:52.880
You get to pick in which order you're going
to prioritize them in.

00:38:52.880 --> 00:39:01.090
And when I went to college, and I took exactly
one econ course, Econ 101, and of course you

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:06.600
learn about Adam Smith and we studied Milton
Friedman, the guy from the University of Chicago,

00:39:06.600 --> 00:39:11.569
and he was like, "Take care of the investor
and everything else takes care of itself."

00:39:11.569 --> 00:39:18.540
So I had that voice on my shoulder, but also
one of the first jobs I had after college,

00:39:18.540 --> 00:39:25.170
I was the Cook County Field Coordinator for
John Anderson's independent run at the presidency

00:39:25.170 --> 00:39:27.079
in 1980.

00:39:27.079 --> 00:39:33.230
And leave it to me to work for the independent
guy, not the Republican, not the Democrat.

00:39:33.230 --> 00:39:38.730
But a hundred percent of the people who reported
to me — that job, by the way, I got 214

00:39:38.730 --> 00:39:42.839
bucks a week, but a hundred percent of the
people who reported to me were volunteers,

00:39:42.839 --> 00:39:47.100
and most of them were older than I was because
I was only 22.

00:39:47.100 --> 00:39:50.470
And I didn't have any way to motivate them
with money.

00:39:50.470 --> 00:39:53.780
I couldn't give them a raise, couldn't dock
them their pay.

00:39:53.780 --> 00:39:58.680
So I learned such a crucial lesson, which
is that, if someone's volunteering, the only

00:39:58.680 --> 00:40:01.119
way to motivate them is to have a higher purpose.

00:40:01.119 --> 00:40:05.680
We're all doing this because we agree with
this bigger idea.

00:40:05.680 --> 00:40:14.089
And so I basically took that idea to work
with me when I first became a restaurateur.

00:40:14.089 --> 00:40:18.540
And, again, everybody was getting paid, but
at least half the people were older than I

00:40:18.540 --> 00:40:24.380
was, and I was learning what it was like to
be a leader, to be the boss.

00:40:24.380 --> 00:40:32.770
And I basically, to this day, treat all of
our employees as if they are volunteers, which

00:40:32.770 --> 00:40:33.770
not in the real sense.

00:40:33.770 --> 00:40:35.000
You're going to get paid.

00:40:35.000 --> 00:40:40.940
But if you're working for me, it means you're
probably good enough to have gotten another

00:40:40.940 --> 00:40:43.990
25 job offers at least.

00:40:43.990 --> 00:40:47.510
And so, as far as I'm concerned, you're volunteering
to share your gifts with us.

00:40:47.510 --> 00:40:52.250
I better give you a higher purpose and reason
for wanting to be here.

00:40:52.250 --> 00:40:58.790
And that's when it became clear to me that
our first stakeholder had to be our own employees.

00:40:58.790 --> 00:41:03.420
And by the way, I look at this, Tim, like
it's a virtuous cycle.

00:41:03.420 --> 00:41:09.369
It's not a totem pole where the employee's
on the top and the investor's on the very

00:41:09.369 --> 00:41:11.160
bottom.

00:41:11.160 --> 00:41:16.000
It's a virtuous cycle where one input leads
to something even better.

00:41:16.000 --> 00:41:20.900
So if you want to have really happy customers,
they shouldn't be the input.

00:41:20.900 --> 00:41:25.650
You should have really happy employees, which
I think then leads to a greater chance you're

00:41:25.650 --> 00:41:27.050
going to have really happy customers.

00:41:27.050 --> 00:41:31.740
If you want to have really happy investors,
you wouldn't want that to be the input, Mr.

00:41:31.740 --> 00:41:32.750
Milton Friedman.

00:41:32.750 --> 00:41:35.119
You'd want that to be the outcome.

00:41:35.119 --> 00:41:40.800
And by the way, what's ultimately the best
way to have happy employees?

00:41:40.800 --> 00:41:44.650
Have really happy investors because that's
the only way people are going to get promotions

00:41:44.650 --> 00:41:46.150
and raises.

00:41:46.150 --> 00:41:52.280
And I've run a business where nobody was getting
promotions and raises and it was not happy.

00:41:52.280 --> 00:41:54.790
We had to go out of business when that happened.

00:41:54.790 --> 00:42:01.599
Tim Ferriss: I'd like to ask you a question
about writing.

00:42:01.599 --> 00:42:05.910
And I'm going to begin with a blast from the
past, which I've kept for a very, very long

00:42:05.910 --> 00:42:11.840
time because I think it's so masterfully crafted,
and it's something that you crafted.

00:42:11.840 --> 00:42:15.580
And this is a polite decline.

00:42:15.580 --> 00:42:16.580
I'm not going to say rejection.

00:42:16.580 --> 00:42:27.000
A polite decline when I, via a mutual acquaintance,
close friend of mine, Jeffrey Zurofsky, reached

00:42:27.000 --> 00:42:31.520
out to see if you would participate in my
book, Tribe of Mentors.

00:42:31.520 --> 00:42:39.180
And the beauty was your response ended up
being incredibly valuable as a polite decline.

00:42:39.180 --> 00:42:46.069
So I'm going to be paraphrasing a bit, but
this is what I received, and it was via Jeffrey

00:42:46.069 --> 00:42:49.109
who was acting as the yenta/intermediary.

00:42:49.109 --> 00:42:52.380
"Jeffrey, greetings and thanks for writing.

00:42:52.380 --> 00:42:56.300
I'm grateful for the invitation to participate
in Tim's next book project, but I'm struggling

00:42:56.300 --> 00:43:00.740
at this moment to make time ends meet for
all we're doing at USHG, including my ongoing

00:43:00.740 --> 00:43:03.010
procrastination with my own writing projects.

00:43:03.010 --> 00:43:07.059
I thought carefully about this, and it's clearly
a wonderful opportunity, but I'm going to

00:43:07.059 --> 00:43:08.420
decline with gratitude.

00:43:08.420 --> 00:43:11.080
Know the book will be a big success!

00:43:11.080 --> 00:43:12.080
Thanks again.

00:43:12.080 --> 00:43:13.080
Danny."

00:43:13.080 --> 00:43:23.240
So this is so noteworthy for me because there
are many ways you can decline something that

00:43:23.240 --> 00:43:27.940
are likely to upset someone or don't do anything
to offset the potential for someone being

00:43:27.940 --> 00:43:28.940
upset.

00:43:28.940 --> 00:43:34.810
I came away from receiving this just laughing
and wanting to get better at writing polite

00:43:34.810 --> 00:43:36.221
declines because I felt better.

00:43:36.221 --> 00:43:43.030
I felt better about you, I respected you more
after receiving that.

00:43:43.030 --> 00:43:45.760
How did you learn to write?

00:43:45.760 --> 00:43:49.560
How did you improve or develop your written
communication?

00:43:49.560 --> 00:43:50.931
Danny Meyer: I can't believe it.

00:43:50.931 --> 00:43:52.079
I can't believe you pulled that letter out.

00:43:52.079 --> 00:43:54.809
That's wild.

00:43:54.809 --> 00:43:55.809
And you know what?

00:43:55.809 --> 00:43:58.900
I do that almost every day.

00:43:58.900 --> 00:44:02.109
But I wouldn't do it if it weren't genuine.

00:44:02.109 --> 00:44:07.290
I wouldn't have gone to the trouble of saying
all that stuff if it weren't genuine.

00:44:07.290 --> 00:44:09.180
I've always loved writing.

00:44:09.180 --> 00:44:13.450
My dad was an amazing editor.

00:44:13.450 --> 00:44:19.090
He was actually the managing editor of the
newspaper at Princeton, The Daily Princetonian,

00:44:19.090 --> 00:44:24.510
and he took great pride in marking up anything
I ever wrote.

00:44:24.510 --> 00:44:26.270
And I care.

00:44:26.270 --> 00:44:27.329
I love writing.

00:44:27.329 --> 00:44:29.520
I love expressing myself.

00:44:29.520 --> 00:44:34.670
But also, I think more to your point, it's
not the quality of the writing as much as

00:44:34.670 --> 00:44:36.750
it is the sentiment behind it, which is that
—

00:44:36.750 --> 00:44:38.620
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

00:44:38.620 --> 00:44:41.220
Danny Meyer: — gets back to what we were
saying earlier.

00:44:41.220 --> 00:44:46.250
If somebody cared enough to reach out their
hand and say, "I want to shake hands with

00:44:46.250 --> 00:44:48.630
you," that's what that was.

00:44:48.630 --> 00:44:52.079
Or they reach out to give you a hug, what
are you going to do?

00:44:52.079 --> 00:44:55.859
Just become a tree and not hug them back?

00:44:55.859 --> 00:44:59.090
And I think that I've learned a very, very
important lesson.

00:44:59.090 --> 00:45:08.780
In fact, I'd be curious, and you'll probably
know, I care so much about letting someone

00:45:08.780 --> 00:45:14.280
know, if I do appreciate an invitation, but
I just genuinely can't do it, I care that

00:45:14.280 --> 00:45:19.440
they know that their invitation mattered to
me so much so that I sometimes procrastinate

00:45:19.440 --> 00:45:27.630
writing that note and learn the hard way that
sometimes my mind and my heart are at war

00:45:27.630 --> 00:45:28.830
with each other.

00:45:28.830 --> 00:45:31.160
My mind knows I shouldn't do it.

00:45:31.160 --> 00:45:33.570
My heart really wants to do it.

00:45:33.570 --> 00:45:39.809
And if that translates to making you or Jeffrey
wait too long to get that response, it doesn't

00:45:39.809 --> 00:45:41.560
really matter what I wrote.

00:45:41.560 --> 00:45:46.660
So I'm really curious to know when Jeff's
invitation came and how many days went by

00:45:46.660 --> 00:45:48.240
before I responded.

00:45:48.240 --> 00:45:50.390
My bet is more days than there should have
been.

00:45:50.390 --> 00:45:55.900
Tim Ferriss: I don't have the dates, but I'll
take a look.

00:45:55.900 --> 00:46:00.230
Has your approach to that type of polite decline
changed over time?

00:46:00.230 --> 00:46:02.650
Do you have other language that you like to
use?

00:46:02.650 --> 00:46:07.079
And, again, this is not to imply, to your
point earlier, that this is disingenuous.

00:46:07.079 --> 00:46:10.170
The assumption is, and I believe, that it's
genuine, but have you found —

00:46:10.170 --> 00:46:11.170
Danny Meyer: And it's really hard.

00:46:11.170 --> 00:46:12.359
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it is.

00:46:12.359 --> 00:46:13.609
Danny Meyer: It's really hard.

00:46:13.609 --> 00:46:20.599
It takes time because what most people do,
sadly, is they just delete the invitation

00:46:20.599 --> 00:46:23.560
or they just ignore it.

00:46:23.560 --> 00:46:26.569
So I think it kind of falls into a couple
camps here.

00:46:26.569 --> 00:46:31.680
So number one is, is this something I really
want to do?

00:46:31.680 --> 00:46:35.260
Number two, if I really want to do it, can
I do it?

00:46:35.260 --> 00:46:36.420
So that's easy.

00:46:36.420 --> 00:46:38.760
Look at your calendar.

00:46:38.760 --> 00:46:46.220
And number three, is this something that I
feel like I should do?

00:46:46.220 --> 00:46:52.490
And then, of the shoulds, there's another
couple buckets, which is is it a should because

00:46:52.490 --> 00:46:58.450
I have to, or is it a should because it's
good?

00:46:58.450 --> 00:47:01.430
I've gotten into most trouble on the shoulds.

00:47:01.430 --> 00:47:05.010
If I really, really want to do something,
like I really wanted to have this conversation

00:47:05.010 --> 00:47:08.569
with you today, I'm the guy that reached out
to you.

00:47:08.569 --> 00:47:15.119
And I love when I get a spark of something
that I really want to do.

00:47:15.119 --> 00:47:21.720
But many, many times, the invitations come
in, I get a lot from politicians, we support

00:47:21.720 --> 00:47:29.760
this or that, I get a lot from speaking opportunities,
et cetera.

00:47:29.760 --> 00:47:30.760
And I'm not complaining.

00:47:30.760 --> 00:47:33.240
I'm very, very fortunate.

00:47:33.240 --> 00:47:38.600
But if it's a should, I learned this lesson
from the restaurateur Jeremy King, who's in

00:47:38.600 --> 00:47:39.600
London.

00:47:39.600 --> 00:47:40.600
Great guy.

00:47:40.600 --> 00:47:45.840
And he said, "The shoulds are what have gotten
me in the most trouble."

00:47:45.840 --> 00:47:48.660
And I ask myself a simple question.

00:47:48.660 --> 00:47:55.000
If this thing were tonight, because it's generally
something four, five, six months from now,

00:47:55.000 --> 00:47:59.260
but if this thing were tonight, is this something
I would be excited to do or would I roll my

00:47:59.260 --> 00:48:03.119
eyes and go, "Oh, man, look what I have to
do tonight."

00:48:03.119 --> 00:48:07.950
And so by making it in the very present, that's
really helped me a lot with the shoulds.

00:48:07.950 --> 00:48:13.559
And then I just feel like it's not my obligation
to say yes to everybody, but I do care.

00:48:13.559 --> 00:48:14.910
I genuinely care.

00:48:14.910 --> 00:48:19.790
If somebody had the courtesy to invite me
to do something, they deserve the courtesy

00:48:19.790 --> 00:48:23.110
of a gracious response.

00:48:23.110 --> 00:48:26.520
All they're guilty of is saying, "I'm interested
in you."

00:48:26.520 --> 00:48:27.770
How bad is that?

00:48:27.770 --> 00:48:28.770
That's a compliment.

00:48:28.770 --> 00:48:35.200
Tim Ferriss: It can be really challenging,
and I just want to give you kudos again for

00:48:35.200 --> 00:48:42.310
an incredibly beautifully crafted polite decline
that I was, might sound bizarre, but thrilled

00:48:42.310 --> 00:48:43.310
to receive.

00:48:43.310 --> 00:48:48.260
I really admired it so much that I've kept
it on hand.

00:48:48.260 --> 00:48:53.859
And I ask about writing because I find that
you seem to pay a lot of attention to language

00:48:53.859 --> 00:48:58.470
and communication and clarity.

00:48:58.470 --> 00:49:03.349
And my attention was drawn, in doing homework
for this conversation, to questions.

00:49:03.349 --> 00:49:06.880
Now you can't believe everything that you
read on the internet, so I do want to fact

00:49:06.880 --> 00:49:13.790
check this, but I found a list of six qualities
Danny Meyer looks for when hiring, that is.

00:49:13.790 --> 00:49:20.280
And the first is, I'm just going to read through
these and then you can correct as needed or

00:49:20.280 --> 00:49:24.720
expand as needed, but I'm going to get to
a few at the end, there are only six, that

00:49:24.720 --> 00:49:25.829
have questions associated.

00:49:25.829 --> 00:49:27.369
So number one: Kind eyes.

00:49:27.369 --> 00:49:30.830
"Eyes don't lie, kind eyes is a hell of a
start!"

00:49:30.830 --> 00:49:31.830
Curiosity.

00:49:31.830 --> 00:49:34.180
Does this person see themselves as a finished
product or are they looking to continually

00:49:34.180 --> 00:49:35.180
learn?

00:49:35.180 --> 00:49:36.440
Three: Work ethic.

00:49:36.440 --> 00:49:40.430
You can teach someone how to decant a bottle
of wine but can't teach them to see opportunities

00:49:40.430 --> 00:49:41.430
to do more.

00:49:41.430 --> 00:49:43.040
Now we're going to get into some questions.

00:49:43.040 --> 00:49:45.420
So four: Empathy.

00:49:45.420 --> 00:49:48.849
Is this the kind of person the entire team
is going to want to be around?

00:49:48.849 --> 00:49:52.540
Ask: "On a scale of 1 - 10, tell me how lucky
are you?"

00:49:52.540 --> 00:49:54.960
So I'm curious about that.

00:49:54.960 --> 00:49:55.960
Self-awareness.

00:49:55.960 --> 00:49:57.569
Can this person read their own weather report?

00:49:57.569 --> 00:50:01.150
Ask: "What is the single biggest misconception
people have about you?"

00:50:01.150 --> 00:50:02.150
And then integrity/trust.

00:50:02.150 --> 00:50:05.290
You ask, "Name something that happened to
you before the age of 12 that has changed

00:50:05.290 --> 00:50:07.619
your life forever."

00:50:07.619 --> 00:50:12.900
If you were hiring for your first restaurant
today, would you ask some of these questions,

00:50:12.900 --> 00:50:16.040
look for these six things, or would you modify
this?

00:50:16.040 --> 00:50:18.890
Danny Meyer: I would absolutely look for those
things.

00:50:18.890 --> 00:50:24.680
In fact, I've checked them with myself year
after year, and there's not one of them I

00:50:24.680 --> 00:50:25.680
would take off the list.

00:50:25.680 --> 00:50:34.609
I want to work with people who are kind people,
who genuinely are optimistic people.

00:50:34.609 --> 00:50:40.580
I'm not really excited working with skeptics
who just see what could go wrong all the time.

00:50:40.580 --> 00:50:42.750
You do need to surround yourself with, look,
I'm a cock-eyed optimist.

00:50:42.750 --> 00:50:48.890
I'm so damn optimist that I basically see
the wine glass as half full even before I

00:50:48.890 --> 00:50:53.680
pull the cork on the bottle, but it's been
important for me to surround myself with people

00:50:53.680 --> 00:50:56.120
who at least ask me some tough questions.

00:50:56.120 --> 00:50:57.859
So I want kind optimists.

00:50:57.859 --> 00:51:03.049
I definitely want curious people who look
at each — I don't want know-it-alls.

00:51:03.049 --> 00:51:04.740
I want learn-it-alls.

00:51:04.740 --> 00:51:06.490
I don't want someone who's already a finished
product.

00:51:06.490 --> 00:51:10.920
It's not fun, especially if you're trying
to make your business better every day.

00:51:10.920 --> 00:51:13.910
I definitely want people who have an excellent
work ethic.

00:51:13.910 --> 00:51:21.619
I don't mean to the point of being sick because
we can all push it too far, but it's not fun

00:51:21.619 --> 00:51:26.089
to be on a team where everybody's really bringing
their best and there's a couple people just

00:51:26.089 --> 00:51:27.930
putting in a C-level effort.

00:51:27.930 --> 00:51:29.530
That doesn't work.

00:51:29.530 --> 00:51:34.530
And I definitely want empathetic people, people
who understand the wake they leave in their

00:51:34.530 --> 00:51:40.140
path, who care about how they make other people
feel just as much as they care how other people

00:51:40.140 --> 00:51:41.140
feel.

00:51:41.140 --> 00:51:46.020
So, yeah, and definitely I want self-aware
people and I want people with integrity.

00:51:46.020 --> 00:51:48.270
So a hundred percent of those.

00:51:48.270 --> 00:51:57.730
The one thing I would add that has really,
really come to light for me is I want people

00:51:57.730 --> 00:52:05.299
who just love to win because that's not really
captured in any of the six that I mentioned

00:52:05.299 --> 00:52:06.299
earlier.

00:52:06.299 --> 00:52:08.730
Wanting to be a champion.

00:52:08.730 --> 00:52:11.230
You look at the best athletes in sports.

00:52:11.230 --> 00:52:18.300
Sure, they have God-given physical ability,
but they also had to train like crazy.

00:52:18.300 --> 00:52:24.450
You don't get to be the Kentucky Derby winner
without — you've got your blood lines, but

00:52:24.450 --> 00:52:26.760
you also had to train like crazy.

00:52:26.760 --> 00:52:32.600
You don't get to be Serena Williams without
the blood lines, but you don't also get to

00:52:32.600 --> 00:52:36.250
be Serena Williams without working incredibly
hard.

00:52:36.250 --> 00:52:37.250
Or Michael Jordan.

00:52:37.250 --> 00:52:38.920
Whoever it is you want to talk about.

00:52:38.920 --> 00:52:40.480
So I look for that.

00:52:40.480 --> 00:52:45.540
I'm really interested to know the motivation
behind what makes someone competitive.

00:52:45.540 --> 00:52:47.599
And I basically have four buckets.

00:52:47.599 --> 00:52:50.900
First of all, there's acompetitive people.

00:52:50.900 --> 00:52:53.300
They're great people.

00:52:53.300 --> 00:52:56.760
They just don't wake up every day saying,
"I've got to win.

00:52:56.760 --> 00:52:58.970
I'm dying to win."

00:52:58.970 --> 00:53:03.520
Tim Ferriss did not get to be Tim Ferriss
without desiring to be the best.

00:53:03.520 --> 00:53:04.520
I know that.

00:53:04.520 --> 00:53:05.520
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I'm very competitive.

00:53:05.520 --> 00:53:06.520
Danny Meyer: That doesn't necessarily come
across, but —

00:53:06.520 --> 00:53:07.520
Danny Meyer: I know that.

00:53:07.520 --> 00:53:08.520
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, very competitive.

00:53:08.520 --> 00:53:09.520
Danny Meyer: And that doesn't necessarily
come across, but you're not a competitive,

00:53:09.520 --> 00:53:10.520
I can tell you that.

00:53:10.520 --> 00:53:14.839
Then the other three buckets I look for, and
this helps me with someone's self-awareness

00:53:14.839 --> 00:53:21.790
as well, is to understand, assuming that you
are motivated to be a champion at what you

00:53:21.790 --> 00:53:28.089
do, what is your primary motivation for wanting
to compete to win?

00:53:28.089 --> 00:53:30.559
And I find there's basically three buckets.

00:53:30.559 --> 00:53:35.130
Sometimes people have a little bit of each,
but there's the — you know that picture

00:53:35.130 --> 00:53:39.079
of Muhammad Ali standing triumphantly over
Sonny Liston —

00:53:39.079 --> 00:53:40.079
Tim Ferriss: I do.

00:53:40.079 --> 00:53:42.369
Danny Meyer: — whose back is on the mat
with his fist up in the air?

00:53:42.369 --> 00:53:49.150
So I can pretty much tell you that Muhammad
Ali was primarily motivated by a love for

00:53:49.150 --> 00:53:50.770
beating someone else.

00:53:50.770 --> 00:53:53.490
That felt really good to him.

00:53:53.490 --> 00:53:56.390
That's not a bad thing or a good thing, it
just is what it is.

00:53:56.390 --> 00:54:02.220
Then you've got, imagine this photograph of
John McEnroe with his headband on and his

00:54:02.220 --> 00:54:09.530
long curly hair, yelling at the umpire, "You've
got to be kidding me."

00:54:09.530 --> 00:54:11.339
That man hated to lose.

00:54:11.339 --> 00:54:19.540
It wasn't so much, "I'm motivated by who I'm
going to beat," it's like, "I will not be

00:54:19.540 --> 00:54:20.540
seen losing."

00:54:20.540 --> 00:54:28.790
And then, the third image I would have would
be a great Olympian, let's say Usain Bolt,

00:54:28.790 --> 00:54:36.000
and his leg is outstretched with the veins
popping, trying to get that extra inch or

00:54:36.000 --> 00:54:40.260
that extra half second off of his time or
whatever.

00:54:40.260 --> 00:54:44.890
And that guy is, he's out there, his primary
motivation, he wants to exceed his own personal

00:54:44.890 --> 00:54:45.890
best.

00:54:45.890 --> 00:54:47.340
He's competing with himself.

00:54:47.340 --> 00:54:54.109
So by knowing these questions, this is something
that I would absolutely want to add today,

00:54:54.109 --> 00:54:59.720
because I know that I want to be the best
and I can't do it by myself.

00:54:59.720 --> 00:55:06.299
So I've got to stock my team with people who
look at every day as an opportunity, not for

00:55:06.299 --> 00:55:11.670
perfection, because I think perfection is
stupid, it's impossible, it's a recipe for

00:55:11.670 --> 00:55:12.670
unhappiness.

00:55:12.670 --> 00:55:17.970
But I do look for people who look at every
day as an opportunity to honor whatever they

00:55:17.970 --> 00:55:20.910
did yesterday, and figure out how to do it
a little bit better today.

00:55:20.910 --> 00:55:22.670
That's the journey of excellence.

00:55:22.670 --> 00:55:27.080
Tim Ferriss: How do you assess, this might
sound silly, work ethic?

00:55:27.080 --> 00:55:28.950
Is it by trialing someone?

00:55:28.950 --> 00:55:32.770
Because everyone's going to be on best behavior
and saying, "Oh, my biggest flaw is I just

00:55:32.770 --> 00:55:33.859
work too hard."

00:55:33.859 --> 00:55:37.470
That type of college application nonsense
in a job interview.

00:55:37.470 --> 00:55:43.380
Do you have particular ways that you would
approach assessing that proactive work ethic?

00:55:43.380 --> 00:55:47.890
Not just doing what you give them, but thinking
about what else they could do?

00:55:47.890 --> 00:55:50.410
How do you think about assessing?

00:55:50.410 --> 00:55:56.299
Danny Meyer: Well, I basically define it as,
you've now learned how to do the job, but

00:55:56.299 --> 00:56:01.870
only you can determine if it matters to you
to do it as well as it can be done.

00:56:01.870 --> 00:56:04.950
And you can see it in people.

00:56:04.950 --> 00:56:07.670
There's so many ways we can all take shortcuts.

00:56:07.670 --> 00:56:10.339
The obvious things are, did you show up?

00:56:10.339 --> 00:56:11.950
Did you show up on time?

00:56:11.950 --> 00:56:17.480
Did you show up shaven, if that's what your
job is in the dining room, let's say?

00:56:17.480 --> 00:56:23.559
Did you press your shirt, or just take it
easy and get that extra third use out of your

00:56:23.559 --> 00:56:28.030
shirt where you don't really care what it
looks like?

00:56:28.030 --> 00:56:34.250
And you see this in sports all the time, and
I think sports has so much to teach us.

00:56:34.250 --> 00:56:37.970
Number one is, I think, in my mind, hospitality
is a team sport.

00:56:37.970 --> 00:56:39.910
We rely on each other.

00:56:39.910 --> 00:56:43.809
It doesn't matter whether you had a bad night
or not, you're not expected to go strike out

00:56:43.809 --> 00:56:45.140
if you're a baseball player.

00:56:45.140 --> 00:56:49.470
It doesn't matter whether you woke up on the
wrong side of the bed or not, you're not expected

00:56:49.470 --> 00:56:52.310
to let a ball roll through your legs at shortstop.

00:56:52.310 --> 00:56:54.720
Well, it's the same thing in my business.

00:56:54.720 --> 00:57:00.390
Your job is to make the rest of your team
better, and your job is to make the rest of

00:57:00.390 --> 00:57:02.641
your team better, whether you're on the field
or in the dugout.

00:57:02.641 --> 00:57:07.770
And I've got a quick story that I learned
so much from other people.

00:57:07.770 --> 00:57:08.770
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, please.

00:57:08.770 --> 00:57:10.359
Danny Meyer: You've probably heard of Theo
Epstein.

00:57:10.359 --> 00:57:13.050
In fact, maybe you've even interviewed him
at some point.

00:57:13.050 --> 00:57:14.910
Tim Ferriss: I have not actually.

00:57:14.910 --> 00:57:16.609
I'm going to plead ignorance.

00:57:16.609 --> 00:57:18.289
I will broadcast it.

00:57:18.289 --> 00:57:25.010
Danny Meyer: So Theo Epstein is the youngest
general manager in, I think, the history of

00:57:25.010 --> 00:57:26.710
Major League Baseball.

00:57:26.710 --> 00:57:31.849
And he was hired at a very, very young age
to take over being the general manager of

00:57:31.849 --> 00:57:38.100
the Boston Red Sox, who had not won anything
for decades and decades and decades.

00:57:38.100 --> 00:57:42.640
And they talked about the Yankee Curse or
whatever the hell it was, but for whatever

00:57:42.640 --> 00:57:44.099
reason, they couldn't win.

00:57:44.099 --> 00:57:48.780
He comes, he's the general manager, revamps
the team, and of course, they win their first

00:57:48.780 --> 00:57:49.780
title.

00:57:49.780 --> 00:57:54.090
I want to say it was in 2004.

00:57:54.090 --> 00:57:57.400
Actually, I don't want to say that because
if that's the case, it was against my St.

00:57:57.400 --> 00:57:58.400
Louis Cardinals.

00:57:58.400 --> 00:58:02.780
But he then went on to go win a couple others
for them, and then he leaves to go to the

00:58:02.780 --> 00:58:04.880
Chicago Cubs.

00:58:04.880 --> 00:58:06.839
And the Chicago Cubs, guess what?

00:58:06.839 --> 00:58:10.280
They have not won a World Series forever.

00:58:10.280 --> 00:58:16.180
He revamps their team, Chicago Cubs win a
World Series for the first time in all these

00:58:16.180 --> 00:58:17.180
years.

00:58:17.180 --> 00:58:24.920
So now, this wonder, Theo Epstein, is asked
by a lot of people, and he basically, "What's

00:58:24.920 --> 00:58:25.920
the secret here?"

00:58:25.920 --> 00:58:29.280
And you've got to understand, baseball is
a game of statistics.

00:58:29.280 --> 00:58:31.339
They measure everything.

00:58:31.339 --> 00:58:36.950
There's statistics I don't even begin to understand,
but 100 percent of those statistics are what's

00:58:36.950 --> 00:58:39.349
happening on the field.

00:58:39.349 --> 00:58:45.990
So running, caught stealing, your fielding
percentage, your batting percentage, your

00:58:45.990 --> 00:58:51.760
batting percentage with runners on base, your
batting percentage against left-handers, right-handers,

00:58:51.760 --> 00:58:53.460
on and on and on and on.

00:58:53.460 --> 00:58:57.760
Half the game of baseball is played while
you're sitting on the bench.

00:58:57.760 --> 00:59:01.670
Your team is in the field, you're on the field.

00:59:01.670 --> 00:59:04.700
When your team is at bat, you're on the bench.

00:59:04.700 --> 00:59:10.500
And so, the real question is, how do you measure
what your impact is on the rest of the team

00:59:10.500 --> 00:59:12.299
when you're in the dugout?

00:59:12.299 --> 00:59:15.369
Did you help the rest of your teammates get
better?

00:59:15.369 --> 00:59:18.839
So I've been watching this guy, Theo Epstein,
trying to learn from him.

00:59:18.839 --> 00:59:22.089
And finally, I have the chance to meet him
once.

00:59:22.089 --> 00:59:27.760
And he had given a talk at a conference I
attended, and it was the year that there was

00:59:27.760 --> 00:59:31.700
a big hurricane in Houston.

00:59:31.700 --> 00:59:35.569
I want to say it was Hurricane Maria, but
I'm probably wrong about that.

00:59:35.569 --> 00:59:39.720
And this was a week after the World Series.

00:59:39.720 --> 00:59:46.990
Houston won the World Series that year, beating
the Los Angeles Dodgers.

00:59:46.990 --> 00:59:51.170
And he was still with the Cubs, the Cubs had
lost to the Dodgers in the Championship Series

00:59:51.170 --> 00:59:52.550
that year.

00:59:52.550 --> 00:59:58.180
So the team that the Cubs lost to, went on
into the World Series and lost to Houston.

00:59:58.180 --> 01:00:02.299
So I go up to Theo Epstein, and I finally
get a chance to talk to this guy who I've

01:00:02.299 --> 01:00:07.950
been learning from from afar, and I ask him
the stupidest question in the world, and I

01:00:07.950 --> 01:00:10.089
go, "Who were you rooting for?

01:00:10.089 --> 01:00:12.470
I've got to know, who were you rooting for
in the World Series?

01:00:12.470 --> 01:00:15.850
Were you rooting for Houston because you felt
bad for the city, because they just had this

01:00:15.850 --> 01:00:20.630
hurricane and you wanted to see something
nice happen for Houston?

01:00:20.630 --> 01:00:24.020
Or were you rooting for the Dodgers because
it would make the Cubs look better if the

01:00:24.020 --> 01:00:27.991
team they lost to went on to become the champion?"

01:00:27.991 --> 01:00:33.940
And he looks at me like I'm from Mars, and
he goes, actually very politely, he goes,

01:00:33.940 --> 01:00:36.309
"Actually, neither one of those things.

01:00:36.309 --> 01:00:41.310
I was absolutely rooting for the Dodgers to
win, but not because it would make the Cubs

01:00:41.310 --> 01:00:42.369
look better.

01:00:42.369 --> 01:00:46.869
It's because, if the Dodgers win the World
Series, I know that the Cubs have to face

01:00:46.869 --> 01:00:49.390
them eight times next year during the season.

01:00:49.390 --> 01:00:53.829
And if they win the World Series, they're
going to be like every other team that wins

01:00:53.829 --> 01:00:57.510
the championship, and they're not going to
do the things they need in the off season

01:00:57.510 --> 01:01:02.250
to improve their team, and that's going to
make them easier for us to compete with next

01:01:02.250 --> 01:01:03.250
year."

01:01:03.250 --> 01:01:06.160
And I went, "Shit, that is such a great lesson."

01:01:06.160 --> 01:01:11.510
Because it's like, when you think you're doing
well or you think you're on top of whatever

01:01:11.510 --> 01:01:15.730
profession, that's the time you've got to
break the glass and you've got to start over.

01:01:15.730 --> 01:01:16.730
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:01:16.730 --> 01:01:20.690
Danny Meyer: And that's when I think most
of us, and it's not just laziness and it's

01:01:20.690 --> 01:01:25.000
not just sitting on your laurels, you think
it's great.

01:01:25.000 --> 01:01:28.680
But the world keeps moving, and if you don't
keep moving with it, you will definitely not

01:01:28.680 --> 01:01:29.680
keep up.

01:01:29.680 --> 01:01:32.069
Tim Ferriss: That is a great story.

01:01:32.069 --> 01:01:39.349
And I'd be curious to know, did you dig into
or have a chance to study how he assessed

01:01:39.349 --> 01:01:44.290
things that were not captured in the usual
stats, people showing up early to practice,

01:01:44.290 --> 01:01:48.400
maybe staying later, practicing A, B, or C?

01:01:48.400 --> 01:01:55.380
Were there any other particular approaches
that you've gleaned from studying him or speaking

01:01:55.380 --> 01:01:56.380
with him?

01:01:56.380 --> 01:02:01.500
Danny Meyer: It was all intuitive to him and
it's the stuff I was looking at on our team.

01:02:01.500 --> 01:02:07.560
The character, like in my business, this is
going to sound really trite, but our staff,

01:02:07.560 --> 01:02:13.020
the cooks and the servers sit down and have
what's called family meal before every lunch

01:02:13.020 --> 01:02:15.579
service and before every dinner service.

01:02:15.579 --> 01:02:20.569
And it's an opportunity for people to come
together, stop.

01:02:20.569 --> 01:02:22.369
We call it family meal.

01:02:22.369 --> 01:02:30.640
We are a business, not a family, but you can
watch during that time, you can just watch

01:02:30.640 --> 01:02:36.650
who's bringing motivating thoughts to the
table, who's actually bringing outside ideas

01:02:36.650 --> 01:02:40.130
to the table, who's asking questions.

01:02:40.130 --> 01:02:42.680
You can tell who's bringing the conversation
down.

01:02:42.680 --> 01:02:46.770
There's always someone on every team it who's
the, "Ain't it bad?"

01:02:46.770 --> 01:02:47.770
person.

01:02:47.770 --> 01:02:48.770
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:02:48.770 --> 01:02:51.410
Danny Meyer: That's not someone we really
want in the dugout.

01:02:51.410 --> 01:02:55.150
I want people who are like, "Just imagine
if we..."

01:02:55.150 --> 01:03:00.520
I want that person, "Just imagine if we could
do this," kind of person.

01:03:00.520 --> 01:03:06.400
But I didn't study that in Theo, except I've
watched how — before Theo Epstein, the most

01:03:06.400 --> 01:03:13.049
famous general manager was a guy named Billy
Beane who famously rebuilt the Oakland As.

01:03:13.049 --> 01:03:14.309
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:03:14.309 --> 01:03:16.930
The antagonist of Moneyball for people who
might [inaudible].

01:03:16.930 --> 01:03:17.930
Danny Meyer: Exactly.

01:03:17.930 --> 01:03:24.349
And I think what Theo did was, he added the
emotional aspect to the technical aspect.

01:03:24.349 --> 01:03:26.430
Tim Ferriss: Mmm.

01:03:26.430 --> 01:03:30.700
So let's say you have someone on your team
you don't want in the dugout, and let's say

01:03:30.700 --> 01:03:37.940
there's a reality TV show where you put on
a Groucho Marx makeup kit, and you go and

01:03:37.940 --> 01:03:39.490
you're starting a new restaurant.

01:03:39.490 --> 01:03:45.470
You can use all the knowledge you have, but
you can't use the contacts, you can't use

01:03:45.470 --> 01:03:50.869
the bankers and the financiers or whatever
you might have had access to before, and you

01:03:50.869 --> 01:03:53.490
have to let somebody go.

01:03:53.490 --> 01:03:55.010
How do you make that decision?

01:03:55.010 --> 01:03:56.349
Is it made quickly?

01:03:56.349 --> 01:04:00.420
Have you set rules for yourself as to how
to go about doing that?

01:04:00.420 --> 01:04:04.150
And then, what is the language or approach
that you might use?

01:04:04.150 --> 01:04:08.330
Danny Meyer: Well, I've gotten much, much
better at it as the years have gone by.

01:04:08.330 --> 01:04:13.020
I'll never forget the first people I had to
fire when I was 27, 28.

01:04:13.020 --> 01:04:19.130
I'd lose sleep for days, literally days.

01:04:19.130 --> 01:04:22.480
I even went on my honeymoon knowing that I
was going to have to fire someone when I came

01:04:22.480 --> 01:04:26.790
back from my honeymoon, and I really regret
that.

01:04:26.790 --> 01:04:30.119
I really regret that I hadn't done it ahead
of time, because that's not something that

01:04:30.119 --> 01:04:33.320
I should have been thinking about on the honeymoon.

01:04:33.320 --> 01:04:37.680
And the early days, I really looked at our
business as if it were a family.

01:04:37.680 --> 01:04:42.680
And again, the way I grew up, it's like my
job was to keep the family together.

01:04:42.680 --> 01:04:48.380
And I would be great at, if somebody wasn't
working out at one position, I'd find a different

01:04:48.380 --> 01:04:51.020
position to move them to.

01:04:51.020 --> 01:04:56.079
Anything to keep them, because it felt like
I had somehow failed if someone was leaving

01:04:56.079 --> 01:04:57.900
our company.

01:04:57.900 --> 01:05:03.079
And I was really, really good, on the other
hand, at rewarding great performance, but

01:05:03.079 --> 01:05:10.200
I was pretty damn bad and/or slow at exiting
people who shouldn't have been on the team.

01:05:10.200 --> 01:05:19.430
And so a big thing happened over time, and
not too far in the rearview mirror either.

01:05:19.430 --> 01:05:25.740
And that was this thing that I've already
alluded to, which is that, number one, it's

01:05:25.740 --> 01:05:27.359
a business, not a family.

01:05:27.359 --> 01:05:34.480
And one of the great things about the restaurant
business is that it does feel family-ish to

01:05:34.480 --> 01:05:40.660
people because you spend so many damn hours
working with each other, but it's not a family.

01:05:40.660 --> 01:05:46.279
And so, it's a good thing, but it's a double-edged
sword, because when you fire somebody from

01:05:46.279 --> 01:05:50.779
the family, also known as your restaurant,
you have to ask yourself, "What will this

01:05:50.779 --> 01:05:54.050
do to the fabric of that group?

01:05:54.050 --> 01:05:56.079
That troop that's working together?"

01:05:56.079 --> 01:06:03.339
So I was able to come up, with the help of
some restaurateurs in California, they gave

01:06:03.339 --> 01:06:08.630
me this model that I just absolutely love,
and we've turned it into something that we

01:06:08.630 --> 01:06:09.630
do.

01:06:09.630 --> 01:06:14.160
And they basically created a four quadrant
thing.

01:06:14.160 --> 01:06:18.740
You've seen these axes many times with a Y-axis
and an X-axis.

01:06:18.740 --> 01:06:24.869
And in one of the quadrants, there's the word,
"Can."

01:06:24.869 --> 01:06:27.059
Let's say the upper left-hand, it says, "Can."

01:06:27.059 --> 01:06:30.559
And then, in the upper right, it says, "Can't."

01:06:30.559 --> 01:06:33.900
And then, in the bottom left, it says, "Will."

01:06:33.900 --> 01:06:36.690
And in the bottom right, it says, "Won't."

01:06:36.690 --> 01:06:42.289
So you basically have someone's technical
abilities, the can and can't, and then you

01:06:42.289 --> 01:06:49.619
have somebody's emotional willingness or aptitude,
and that's will and won't.

01:06:49.619 --> 01:06:54.920
And so what we've been able to do, we've actually
created mirrors, which we've put in the locker

01:06:54.920 --> 01:06:59.970
rooms of our restaurants, because we're not
trying to keep this a secret.

01:06:59.970 --> 01:07:03.569
And the mirror has all four quadrants.

01:07:03.569 --> 01:07:08.220
And so, when you go put on your uniform every
day, you get to look at yourself in the mirror

01:07:08.220 --> 01:07:11.829
if you so choose and see this quadrant.

01:07:11.829 --> 01:07:25.529
So basically, if you have someone who will
but can't, that's a very different thing than

01:07:25.529 --> 01:07:27.970
someone who won't and can't.

01:07:27.970 --> 01:07:28.970
Tim Ferriss: Sure.

01:07:28.970 --> 01:07:35.310
Danny Meyer: Or if you have someone who — I
could go through all of them, but what we've

01:07:35.310 --> 01:07:43.530
basically done, Tim, is that we have an action
point and a timeframe for each one of those.

01:07:43.530 --> 01:07:52.640
So if you've got somebody who can and will,
I want to celebrate that person, I want to

01:07:52.640 --> 01:07:56.800
replicate that, but I really want to pay at
— those are my flowers, I really want to

01:07:56.800 --> 01:07:59.130
water them.

01:07:59.130 --> 01:08:01.630
Too often we ignore those people because,
"Oh, that's easy.

01:08:01.630 --> 01:08:03.920
We don't have to worry about Johnny because
he always gets it right."

01:08:03.920 --> 01:08:04.920
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:08:04.920 --> 01:08:08.290
Danny Meyer: You really want to water those
flowers and celebrate them.

01:08:08.290 --> 01:08:16.830
If you have someone who can't but will, I'm
going to coach them, and I don't mind saying

01:08:16.830 --> 01:08:22.430
this, but the wick on my candle is pretty
long for someone who will.

01:08:22.430 --> 01:08:27.980
Because if you can teach them how to do the
thing and they're willing to do it and they've

01:08:27.980 --> 01:08:33.671
got the right approach, the right hospitality
attitude, once they learn how to do it, you're

01:08:33.671 --> 01:08:37.170
going to have a loyal employee for life because
you stuck with them.

01:08:37.170 --> 01:08:42.230
Now, on the other hand, so let's say that's
a six-month wick on my candle.

01:08:42.230 --> 01:08:46.180
Let's say you've got someone who can't and
won't.

01:08:46.180 --> 01:08:47.259
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:08:47.259 --> 01:08:53.750
Danny Meyer: I'm going to put the candle underneath
their rear end, and they're going to have

01:08:53.750 --> 01:08:57.140
to learn that this isn't working.

01:08:57.140 --> 01:09:01.080
And that's going to be a very, very short,
that's got to be a short window, because the

01:09:01.080 --> 01:09:07.230
longer that person stays on the team, everyone
else on the team says, "Why should I try?

01:09:07.230 --> 01:09:12.959
If they keep batting that person in the lineup,
instead of benching them or sending them to

01:09:12.959 --> 01:09:15.699
the minor leagues, why should I try?"

01:09:15.699 --> 01:09:22.020
The hardest one I find is the can but won't.

01:09:22.020 --> 01:09:26.080
That's the person where you just go, "You're
way better than this, but for some reason,

01:09:26.080 --> 01:09:27.679
you're just choosing not to bring it here."

01:09:27.679 --> 01:09:28.679
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:09:28.679 --> 01:09:32.810
Danny Meyer: And so that's going to have a
pretty short life as well.

01:09:32.810 --> 01:09:38.640
But by actually naming all four of those things,
it really, really helps us to have these conversations.

01:09:38.640 --> 01:09:44.390
And we tell people up front, so you're not
having, they see that mirror, so it's not

01:09:44.390 --> 01:09:46.290
the first time you're having this conversation.

01:09:46.290 --> 01:09:50.759
It gives you a language to say, "Here's where
you are right now, and it's not working."

01:09:50.759 --> 01:09:58.340
And I'll say probably the greatest lesson
I've learned about anything has been really

01:09:58.340 --> 01:10:02.489
trying to understand, how do you scale culture?

01:10:02.489 --> 01:10:08.950
And I've come up with this equation in my
own mind that took me a long time to get to.

01:10:08.950 --> 01:10:15.679
I used to think that by rewarding the behaviors
I wanted, that was the best way to fuel the

01:10:15.679 --> 01:10:17.240
culture I wanted.

01:10:17.240 --> 01:10:23.050
And now, I look at it a little bit differently,
because that's turning a blind eye sometimes

01:10:23.050 --> 01:10:25.390
to the behaviors I don't want.

01:10:25.390 --> 01:10:26.830
Tim Ferriss: Yep.

01:10:26.830 --> 01:10:34.630
Danny Meyer: So I've now learned that the
culture you have in your organization is the

01:10:34.630 --> 01:10:43.620
sum of all the wanted behaviors that you celebrate,
minus all the unwanted behaviors that you

01:10:43.620 --> 01:10:46.400
tolerate.

01:10:46.400 --> 01:10:53.070
And I've learned that the hard way, because
people think I'm full of crap, they can read

01:10:53.070 --> 01:10:56.760
anything I write about our culture, et cetera.

01:10:56.760 --> 01:11:03.650
But if I'm tolerating behaviors that don't
promote either the excellence or wellbeing

01:11:03.650 --> 01:11:10.409
of the team, then everything I've done on
the positive side should be called into question.

01:11:10.409 --> 01:11:18.600
Tim Ferriss: How might you, these days, having
had much more practice since your honeymoon,

01:11:18.600 --> 01:11:23.420
if you had to let somebody go, or you wanted
to maybe suggest languages someone might use,

01:11:23.420 --> 01:11:27.980
but you could make it personal, how might
you phrase that conversation?

01:11:27.980 --> 01:11:31.340
Danny Meyer: Well, first of all, it shouldn't
be the first time you've had the conversation.

01:11:31.340 --> 01:11:32.340
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:11:32.340 --> 01:11:36.510
Danny Meyer: There's nothing worse than when
somebody feels like they got whacked in the

01:11:36.510 --> 01:11:40.960
back of the head, and they're just shocked
because you had never had the conversation.

01:11:40.960 --> 01:11:45.900
You hadn't had the tough conversation that
said, "Either your performance or your behavior,

01:11:45.900 --> 01:11:46.900
it's one of the two…"

01:11:46.900 --> 01:11:47.900
Tim Ferriss: Yep.

01:11:47.900 --> 01:11:49.469
Danny Meyer: "Aren't measuring up."

01:11:49.469 --> 01:11:53.240
And that's another reason that we have to
be really clear about what excellent performance

01:11:53.240 --> 01:11:59.040
looks like, and what wanted behaviors are,
which we're very, very clear about.

01:11:59.040 --> 01:12:05.080
And by being up front with people and not
having this be the first time you're having

01:12:05.080 --> 01:12:08.612
the conversation, it takes a lot of the emotion
out of it.

01:12:08.612 --> 01:12:16.989
It doesn't mean someone's going to be happy,
but it basically sounds like — actually,

01:12:16.989 --> 01:12:24.489
something that I've said on a few occasions,
not in the time I'm actually exiting somebody,

01:12:24.489 --> 01:12:29.179
but in the time leading up to it, is I use
what I call the jigsaw puzzle analogy.

01:12:29.179 --> 01:12:30.750
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.

01:12:30.750 --> 01:12:35.870
Danny Meyer: We've all done jigsaw puzzles,
and the more challenging they are, you get

01:12:35.870 --> 01:12:41.850
to this point where it's starting to take
some shape, but you still have a lot more

01:12:41.850 --> 01:12:47.920
pieces that you haven't put together than
what you have in your little shape there.

01:12:47.920 --> 01:12:53.590
And invariably, you're going to come up with
a piece that looks like it's right, and you

01:12:53.590 --> 01:12:57.170
put it down and it's almost right.

01:12:57.170 --> 01:13:03.330
It's so almost right that you keep jiggering
it around to make it right, even though you

01:13:03.330 --> 01:13:06.280
know it's not right, and the piece knows it's
not right.

01:13:06.280 --> 01:13:12.420
And what starts to happen is, the paper on
top of the jigsaw piece starts to fray a little

01:13:12.420 --> 01:13:13.880
bit.

01:13:13.880 --> 01:13:18.420
And in fact, the jigsaw puzzle, if you keep
trying, starts to fray.

01:13:18.420 --> 01:13:24.610
Well, that's what we do too often with employees
that are almost right, but they're not really

01:13:24.610 --> 01:13:29.970
the right fit, and it ends up not being good
for the puzzle or for the puzzle piece.

01:13:29.970 --> 01:13:35.300
So I try to explain that to people.

01:13:35.300 --> 01:13:41.050
If you get that conversation with me, chances
are probably 80 percent that the next conversation

01:13:41.050 --> 01:13:42.050
is, "You should go.

01:13:42.050 --> 01:13:45.270
You're a beautiful jigsaw piece, but you should
probably be part of a different puzzle."

01:13:45.270 --> 01:13:46.270
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:13:46.270 --> 01:13:50.020
Danny Meyer: It doesn't make you wrong, doesn't
make you bad, you didn't — now, if it's

01:13:50.020 --> 01:13:54.060
someone who actually did something bad, that's
a very different conversation.

01:13:54.060 --> 01:13:56.250
It's like, "You cannot work here.

01:13:56.250 --> 01:14:01.170
You've betrayed integrity, you've betrayed
someone on our team, you've betrayed one of

01:14:01.170 --> 01:14:05.239
our guests, you've betrayed our investors,
whatever."

01:14:05.239 --> 01:14:10.310
If you cross any one of our stakeholders,
that's a very different conversation, lack

01:14:10.310 --> 01:14:11.310
of integrity.

01:14:11.310 --> 01:14:17.219
But if it's just not the right fit, number
one, it shouldn't be the first time they've

01:14:17.219 --> 01:14:18.600
heard about it.

01:14:18.600 --> 01:14:22.900
And they may not love the conversation.

01:14:22.900 --> 01:14:24.190
Who in the world wants to be exited?

01:14:24.190 --> 01:14:25.200
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:14:25.200 --> 01:14:29.760
Danny Meyer: But I'll tell you one thing I
feel really good about is, we have a pretty

01:14:29.760 --> 01:14:37.969
small industry, and invariably, if you're
someone who left our business, on your terms

01:14:37.969 --> 01:14:42.990
or on our terms, you're probably going to
be serving me in a restaurant sometime in

01:14:42.990 --> 01:14:44.970
New York.

01:14:44.970 --> 01:14:48.570
And I feel really good that I never mind seeing
these people.

01:14:48.570 --> 01:14:53.110
It just feels like, as long as it's clean
and we were honest with each other, it tends

01:14:53.110 --> 01:14:54.580
to work out in the end.

01:14:54.580 --> 01:15:01.139
Tim Ferriss: Reading your bio, looking at
the highlights, you've had so many successes,

01:15:01.139 --> 01:15:06.860
I would imagine, beyond your wildest dreams
or expectations that you could have had in

01:15:06.860 --> 01:15:09.740
your, say, late 20s.

01:15:09.740 --> 01:15:14.550
What are some of your favorite failures, or
any favorite failures that come to mind?

01:15:14.550 --> 01:15:19.300
And by favorite failures, I mean a failure
that you learned a lot from, that set you

01:15:19.300 --> 01:15:21.989
up unexpectedly for later success.

01:15:21.989 --> 01:15:26.860
Anything that sticks out, comes to mind, as
a seminal moment.

01:15:26.860 --> 01:15:27.860
Danny Meyer: Yeah.

01:15:27.860 --> 01:15:29.650
Tim Ferriss: A teaching moment.

01:15:29.650 --> 01:15:31.860
Danny Meyer: Many, many, many.

01:15:31.860 --> 01:15:34.550
Every day we have micro failures.

01:15:34.550 --> 01:15:37.900
There's a really good movie called The Best
Exotic Marigold Hotel.

01:15:37.900 --> 01:15:39.469
I don't know if you've ever seen it.

01:15:39.469 --> 01:15:40.469
Tim Ferriss: I haven't watched it.

01:15:40.469 --> 01:15:43.550
Danny Meyer: So it's a terrible title because
I can almost never remember it.

01:15:43.550 --> 01:15:48.510
I want to say Carnation, Chrysanthemum, but
it's The Best Exotic Marigold Hotel, takes

01:15:48.510 --> 01:15:50.400
place in India.

01:15:50.400 --> 01:15:55.280
And there's two great lessons from that, one
of which, I think, answers, there's probably

01:15:55.280 --> 01:16:01.070
more than two, but one of my favorite lessons
is that the only real failure is the failure

01:16:01.070 --> 01:16:05.739
to try, and that the measure of success is
how we cope with the disappointment.

01:16:05.739 --> 01:16:07.989
And so —
Tim Ferriss: I like that latter.

01:16:07.989 --> 01:16:15.550
Danny Meyer: I'd say that I was, probably
the first most pivotal experience for me was

01:16:15.550 --> 01:16:22.880
closing the first restaurant I ever closed,
which was Tabla, an Indian restaurant.

01:16:22.880 --> 01:16:26.010
Interesting that I'm using an Indian film
to talk about this.

01:16:26.010 --> 01:16:34.610
But Tabla was about 13 years old, and if I
regret one thing that I wrote in Setting the

01:16:34.610 --> 01:16:38.010
Table, it was actually in the very first paragraph
of the book.

01:16:38.010 --> 01:16:44.180
And I proudly stated, at this point, we had
been in business for about 20 years, and I

01:16:44.180 --> 01:16:47.989
proudly stated how many restaurants we had
opened.

01:16:47.989 --> 01:16:54.230
And then, I super proudly stated, "And in
all that time, we've never closed one," as

01:16:54.230 --> 01:16:57.219
if closing a restaurant is failure.

01:16:57.219 --> 01:17:02.949
And it was the stupidest thing I could have
possibly written because closing a restaurant

01:17:02.949 --> 01:17:10.190
is not failing, and it's also nothing to be
ashamed of and nothing to be proud of to keep

01:17:10.190 --> 01:17:11.659
a restaurant open forever.

01:17:11.659 --> 01:17:15.469
Either it is or it isn't.

01:17:15.469 --> 01:17:25.280
So Tabla, I guarantee you, I was so falsely
proud with what I had written, that I kept

01:17:25.280 --> 01:17:29.679
Tabla — and I was so afraid of my dad's
— I didn't want to go down the path of being

01:17:29.679 --> 01:17:31.250
my dad.

01:17:31.250 --> 01:17:33.929
You telling me that I couldn't make something
work here?

01:17:33.929 --> 01:17:44.040
Tabla was a great Indian expression with,
in a very groundbreaking way in New York City,

01:17:44.040 --> 01:17:45.900
using fresh green market ingredients.

01:17:45.900 --> 01:17:53.900
We had a fantastic chef who sadly died during
Covid, Floyd Cardoz.

01:17:53.900 --> 01:18:00.179
Groundbreaking restaurant, lived for 13 years,
probably should have closed it at year 11.

01:18:00.179 --> 01:18:07.290
We had just hit the Great Recession and we
started to lose money.

01:18:07.290 --> 01:18:11.909
It was our biggest restaurant in terms of
numbers of seats.

01:18:11.909 --> 01:18:15.610
We had 283 seats on three different dining
rooms, two different levels.

01:18:15.610 --> 01:18:21.570
And I was so afraid of closing it, so afraid
of talking to the team and saying we couldn't

01:18:21.570 --> 01:18:26.260
make the restaurant work, that I kept it open
for two years longer than I should have.

01:18:26.260 --> 01:18:32.290
And that was the restaurant where, for two
years, we had people working out of loyalty.

01:18:32.290 --> 01:18:36.239
No one was making a raise, no one was getting
a promotion.

01:18:36.239 --> 01:18:43.890
And I finally gathered the courage to do what
I should have done two years sooner.

01:18:43.890 --> 01:18:50.469
And I'll never forget the day I said, "Look,
what if we could distinguish ourselves as

01:18:50.469 --> 01:18:58.280
much based on how well we closed a restaurant,
as we had with how well we had opened a restaurant?"

01:18:58.280 --> 01:19:02.821
And so we started writing a list of, "All
right, what are all the ways that we could

01:19:02.821 --> 01:19:06.920
look back on this and say we did it the right
way?"

01:19:06.920 --> 01:19:11.010
And first thing was, we told our whole staff.

01:19:11.010 --> 01:19:14.190
Now, that sounds, "Well, why is that such
a big...?"

01:19:14.190 --> 01:19:19.280
Well, sadly, in our business, sometimes the
first time a staff learns a restaurant's going

01:19:19.280 --> 01:19:23.860
out of business is the day they go to work
and see a padlock on the door, because the

01:19:23.860 --> 01:19:27.570
restaurant is dead afraid that no one will
work there.

01:19:27.570 --> 01:19:30.130
No one wants to stay on a sinking ship.

01:19:30.130 --> 01:19:37.800
So we told our staff, quarter of a year ahead
of time, told our landlord, told the community.

01:19:37.800 --> 01:19:44.880
We invited all of the alumni of the restaurant
in to come cook with us, so we celebrated

01:19:44.880 --> 01:19:46.600
the restaurant.

01:19:46.600 --> 01:19:54.290
We hosted job fairs for all of our team members,
not just with us teaching them how to interview

01:19:54.290 --> 01:20:00.560
and working potentially in our other restaurants,
but again, inviting famous chefs who had once

01:20:00.560 --> 01:20:05.870
cooked with us, or general managers who had
once worked with us, to come in and hire our

01:20:05.870 --> 01:20:08.310
staff.

01:20:08.310 --> 01:20:10.800
We hosted three fundraisers.

01:20:10.800 --> 01:20:16.270
We brought in Indian chefs from around the
country to do a fundraiser for earthquake

01:20:16.270 --> 01:20:17.880
victims in India.

01:20:17.880 --> 01:20:20.389
We did a fundraiser for Madison Square Park.

01:20:20.389 --> 01:20:24.100
So by the time this was done, we paid our
landlord everything.

01:20:24.100 --> 01:20:31.280
We actually even paid our investors, I think
they got a 0.1 percent return on their original

01:20:31.280 --> 01:20:32.280
investment.

01:20:32.280 --> 01:20:34.360
They got all their money back, plus a couple
of pennies.

01:20:34.360 --> 01:20:38.679
So if you're going to close a place, at least
that was the way to do it.

01:20:38.679 --> 01:20:43.590
And the biggest learning I had was, there's
no shame in closing.

01:20:43.590 --> 01:20:49.889
Since closing Tabla, I would say we've probably
opened 15 restaurants, and we've probably

01:20:49.889 --> 01:20:53.050
closed another six.

01:20:53.050 --> 01:20:57.940
And we had to close about three of them during
Covid.

01:20:57.940 --> 01:21:03.300
And I think that learning to fail fast, and
realize that if you didn't tried the thing

01:21:03.300 --> 01:21:07.389
in the first place, there would neither have
been a success or failure.

01:21:07.389 --> 01:21:10.989
So the real failure would've been not in trying.

01:21:10.989 --> 01:21:12.870
But not everything has to go on forever.

01:21:12.870 --> 01:21:19.700
The late restaurateur, Joe Baum, had this
great expression that I love and I don't love.

01:21:19.700 --> 01:21:25.429
But he used to say that the definition of
a classic restaurant is one that can outlive

01:21:25.429 --> 01:21:27.540
its original lease.

01:21:27.540 --> 01:21:31.650
And that was a goal of mine for a long, long
time.

01:21:31.650 --> 01:21:35.500
And frankly, we've done that with almost all
of our restaurants.

01:21:35.500 --> 01:21:42.060
I now have a different goal of my own, which
is, I want our restaurants — and I think

01:21:42.060 --> 01:21:47.420
your podcast is this as well, and we all have
songs in our lives that are like this, or

01:21:47.420 --> 01:21:50.400
pieces of art or movies or books.

01:21:50.400 --> 01:21:53.070
But I want our restaurants to become essential
in people's lives.

01:21:53.070 --> 01:21:57.440
I want people to say, "My life got better
because that restaurant existed."

01:21:57.440 --> 01:22:00.900
It doesn't matter how long.

01:22:00.900 --> 01:22:05.340
And God forbid the restaurant goes out of
business, I want people to say, "I just lost

01:22:05.340 --> 01:22:07.520
a little something when that restaurant closed."

01:22:07.520 --> 01:22:11.570
There's so many restaurants, dry cleaners,
whatever.

01:22:11.570 --> 01:22:13.940
They come and go and it's like, "Who cares?"

01:22:13.940 --> 01:22:17.670
There's so many songs, if I never heard it
again, I'd be fine.

01:22:17.670 --> 01:22:23.170
But there's this handful of songs that when
I listen to them, I'm grateful for the person

01:22:23.170 --> 01:22:25.870
who wrote it, I'm grateful for their life.

01:22:25.870 --> 01:22:31.030
My life got better because that song existed,
and I can't even imagine my life if that song

01:22:31.030 --> 01:22:32.420
had never been written.

01:22:32.420 --> 01:22:37.040
That's my, more than longevity, it's essentiality
that I think matters.

01:22:37.040 --> 01:22:42.630
Tim Ferriss: I haven't thought about this
in a really long time, but I still remember

01:22:42.630 --> 01:22:49.590
every restaurant and coffee shop in which
I wrote my books, because these locations,

01:22:49.590 --> 01:22:56.670
and I took great pains to find the right spot,
became my surrogate family for a period of

01:22:56.670 --> 01:23:02.710
time while I worked on these things that of
course became a huge part of my life, continue

01:23:02.710 --> 01:23:03.920
to be a huge part of my life.

01:23:03.920 --> 01:23:09.170
And even though some of them have gone out
of business, or I shouldn't say gone out of

01:23:09.170 --> 01:23:13.380
business, maybe they were just closed, maybe
the decision was made to close, they still

01:23:13.380 --> 01:23:17.840
have left this indelible mark in my mind.

01:23:17.840 --> 01:23:23.440
And that just brought all of these memories
rushing back like the Pixar movie Ratatouille.

01:23:23.440 --> 01:23:26.280
The similar effect just now as you were talking
about that.

01:23:26.280 --> 01:23:34.920
And I admire you for being, in my mind, an
experimentalist, running experiments, being

01:23:34.920 --> 01:23:37.969
willing to try in terms of testing things.

01:23:37.969 --> 01:23:44.090
And I remember, this was some time ago, you
could probably place the timestamp effectively,

01:23:44.090 --> 01:23:49.110
but when you experimented with no tipping.

01:23:49.110 --> 01:23:55.900
And I would love for you to speak to that
and just discuss lessons learned through that.

01:23:55.900 --> 01:24:00.070
And this ties into something we were talking
about a little bit before I recorded, which

01:24:00.070 --> 01:24:03.260
was time in Japan where tipping is not a thing,
as an example.

01:24:03.260 --> 01:24:05.310
It's just not really part of the culture.

01:24:05.310 --> 01:24:09.560
But could you describe what you did and what
you learned?

01:24:09.560 --> 01:24:10.560
Danny Meyer: Yeah.

01:24:10.560 --> 01:24:16.390
We had faced a major, major shortage in really
good cooks.

01:24:16.390 --> 01:24:23.030
This was probably starting in about 2012,
2013, 2014.

01:24:23.030 --> 01:24:28.040
And I'll never forget, I went to one of the
restaurants we closed, which was a really

01:24:28.040 --> 01:24:32.110
good restaurant, but I think we got a subpar
location for it.

01:24:32.110 --> 01:24:34.630
It was called North End Grill.

01:24:34.630 --> 01:24:41.590
And I'll never forget going into the restaurant
one night and I said to our general manager,

01:24:41.590 --> 01:24:49.760
Kevin, I said, "God, service has gotten really
good here and I'm so proud."

01:24:49.760 --> 01:24:54.460
I spoke to four different servers and they
all told me that they had graduated from the

01:24:54.460 --> 01:24:56.140
Culinary Institute of America.

01:24:56.140 --> 01:25:01.790
And I said, "How are we getting so many people
who want to be professional servers?

01:25:01.790 --> 01:25:02.790
This is great."

01:25:02.790 --> 01:25:06.570
And he goes, "Boss, I wish I could tell you
that was the truth."

01:25:06.570 --> 01:25:13.010
He said, "These are all people who wanted
to be cooks, but they can't afford to be cooks.

01:25:13.010 --> 01:25:18.180
And there's like three of them living in a
studio apartment, commuting all the way from

01:25:18.180 --> 01:25:23.500
Queens, and we can't pay them any more money
because we don't have any more money to pay

01:25:23.500 --> 01:25:24.500
them.

01:25:24.500 --> 01:25:29.080
We're trying to give them free Metro cards,
but that's not going to keep them.

01:25:29.080 --> 01:25:32.120
So the only way they can make a living is
to be waiters."

01:25:32.120 --> 01:25:39.429
And something I'd been thinking about for
many, many years was how much I did not like

01:25:39.429 --> 01:25:40.429
the tipping system.

01:25:40.429 --> 01:25:46.030
Initially, in the early part of my career,
I didn't like the tipping system because there

01:25:46.030 --> 01:25:51.020
would always be a situation in these early
days of Union Square Cafe or Gramercy Tavern

01:25:51.020 --> 01:25:59.850
when a tourist from Japan or from France or
Great Britain where there's not a big tipping

01:25:59.850 --> 01:26:06.010
culture would either leave no tip or they'd
leave a pretty shitty tip, and it would be

01:26:06.010 --> 01:26:08.640
so demoralizing for the waiter.

01:26:08.640 --> 01:26:14.989
There was one occasion where one of our waiters
actually chased somebody out onto the sidewalk,

01:26:14.989 --> 01:26:15.989
and I was mortified.

01:26:15.989 --> 01:26:17.610
It's like, "You cannot do that."

01:26:17.610 --> 01:26:24.090
And yet here I was, because the tipping system
where they were getting the adjusted tip minimum

01:26:24.090 --> 01:26:29.420
wage back then, which was $2.21, and if they
didn't get a tip, they weren't going to be

01:26:29.420 --> 01:26:30.420
able to pay their rent.

01:26:30.420 --> 01:26:33.610
So I understood it, but it was just, I hated
it.

01:26:33.610 --> 01:26:42.409
But then later, now bringing it up to the
mid 2015 time, I said, "You know what?

01:26:42.409 --> 01:26:49.400
I'm so tired of this system where we are legally
prohibited from allowing tips to be shared

01:26:49.400 --> 01:26:52.429
between waiters and cooks."

01:26:52.429 --> 01:26:55.440
Cooks work at least as hard as waiters, right?

01:26:55.440 --> 01:27:01.410
The guy who made the risotto, stirring the
risotto like crazy, didn't work any less hard

01:27:01.410 --> 01:27:04.710
than the guy that brought it to the table.

01:27:04.710 --> 01:27:09.830
And by the way, if somebody's going to have
truffles shaved on that risotto, and therefore

01:27:09.830 --> 01:27:13.070
the price is going to go way up, the waiter
is going to make a whole lot more money and

01:27:13.070 --> 01:27:16.020
the cook's going to make zero more money.

01:27:16.020 --> 01:27:22.480
And what I had noticed was that every year,
the waiters were making increasingly more

01:27:22.480 --> 01:27:23.480
money.

01:27:23.480 --> 01:27:24.480
Why?

01:27:24.480 --> 01:27:29.280
Well, because menu prices only go up, and
what's a tip if not a multiplier of the menu

01:27:29.280 --> 01:27:30.409
price.

01:27:30.409 --> 01:27:35.540
And cooks' hourly wages had remained stagnant.

01:27:35.540 --> 01:27:38.750
And so you may say, "Well, why don't you just
raise the cook's wage?"

01:27:38.750 --> 01:27:39.750
Great.

01:27:39.750 --> 01:27:44.409
Every time I raise the cook's wage, I have
to increase the menu prices, which only increases

01:27:44.409 --> 01:27:48.739
the disparity because now the tipped employee
makes more.

01:27:48.739 --> 01:27:55.250
So I was really tired of trying to argue that
New York state or many, many other states

01:27:55.250 --> 01:27:59.590
should change their laws so that tips can
be shared.

01:27:59.590 --> 01:28:01.040
And so I said, "Screw it.

01:28:01.040 --> 01:28:03.150
I'm just going to take this into my own hands."

01:28:03.150 --> 01:28:09.139
And I came to a meeting one day with my senior
leaders and I played one of the worst John

01:28:09.139 --> 01:28:13.739
Lennon songs I've ever heard called "Cold
Turkey."

01:28:13.739 --> 01:28:21.159
And I said, "I am now declaring tips to be
a drug and we've got to get off this drug.

01:28:21.159 --> 01:28:26.159
And if we can't change the rules, we're just
going to stop taking tips."

01:28:26.159 --> 01:28:30.030
And we came up with this idea called Hospitality
Included.

01:28:30.030 --> 01:28:36.450
And my goal was that we would narrow the gap
between what a cook could not make and what

01:28:36.450 --> 01:28:37.560
a server could make.

01:28:37.560 --> 01:28:41.620
I didn't want to punish our servers, but I
wanted our cooks to get to make more money

01:28:41.620 --> 01:28:44.310
so we could attract more cooks.

01:28:44.310 --> 01:28:49.659
Otherwise, New York City as the preeminent
dining capital in America was definitely going

01:28:49.659 --> 01:28:51.070
to be threatened.

01:28:51.070 --> 01:28:58.239
So we established this idea, did some really,
really hard math, and I'm telling you it's

01:28:58.239 --> 01:29:03.580
incredibly hard because you've got three legs
on a stool.

01:29:03.580 --> 01:29:08.010
You've got the consumer who's looking at menu
prices that now include everything.

01:29:08.010 --> 01:29:14.460
So if you go to a typical restaurant in America,
the menu price has to include the cost of

01:29:14.460 --> 01:29:18.940
whatever it is that menu item is, the chicken
and everything that came with it.

01:29:18.940 --> 01:29:24.920
It's got to include the cost of the linen,
the flowers, the rent, the insurance.

01:29:24.920 --> 01:29:30.420
It includes everything except paying the person
who brought it to your table.

01:29:30.420 --> 01:29:34.139
You're going to pay that two and a half hours
after you start your meal.

01:29:34.139 --> 01:29:39.190
You're going to get your bill, and then you're
going to go into your other pocket and add

01:29:39.190 --> 01:29:40.600
20 percent to that.

01:29:40.600 --> 01:29:42.469
But you're going to do it.

01:29:42.469 --> 01:29:47.320
So with Hospitality Included, I said everything's
included.

01:29:47.320 --> 01:29:53.750
And I purposely called it hospitality, not
service, because the way I look at it is I

01:29:53.750 --> 01:29:56.969
want you to feel like you're paying for how
we made you feel.

01:29:56.969 --> 01:30:01.990
I want the food to be free, the drink to be
free, and when you look at that big number

01:30:01.990 --> 01:30:09.100
at the bottom of your thing, you've got to
feel like, man, I just got a $350 hug.

01:30:09.100 --> 01:30:11.570
And so that's why I wanted to call it Hospitality
Included.

01:30:11.570 --> 01:30:13.520
And I wanted our staff to understand that.

01:30:13.520 --> 01:30:18.770
And so the math was really hard because by
the time I included everything on the menu

01:30:18.770 --> 01:30:25.870
price, I don't want to bring our waiters down,
I just want to bring our cooks up by 20 percent.

01:30:25.870 --> 01:30:32.870
Oh, by the way, I also wanted to bring our
starting manager salary up because one of

01:30:32.870 --> 01:30:39.620
the worst things about the tipping system
is that you cannot afford, in most cases,

01:30:39.620 --> 01:30:44.551
to promote yourself from being a great server
to being a manager without taking a 25 percent

01:30:44.551 --> 01:30:46.010
pay cut.

01:30:46.010 --> 01:30:49.180
And that that's really screwed up, that there's
nowhere to grow.

01:30:49.180 --> 01:30:54.850
It's a dead end for waiters unless they're
willing to decide we're going to raise our

01:30:54.850 --> 01:30:56.200
manager's wage.

01:30:56.200 --> 01:31:00.489
Oh, by the way, we're also going to put in
a retirement plan for our team.

01:31:00.489 --> 01:31:06.780
We're going to put in a family leave policy
so that when people are pregnant or people

01:31:06.780 --> 01:31:11.489
have a baby, both the birth mother and the
birth father can get up to eight weeks of

01:31:11.489 --> 01:31:13.030
paid time off.

01:31:13.030 --> 01:31:16.210
We wanted to put all that in the price.

01:31:16.210 --> 01:31:19.550
And we didn't want to scare people away from
dining at our restaurants.

01:31:19.550 --> 01:31:23.440
And we wanted to leave some money for our
investors.

01:31:23.440 --> 01:31:27.100
So we tried it at one restaurant, The Modern.

01:31:27.100 --> 01:31:29.210
Really hard, but it worked pretty well.

01:31:29.210 --> 01:31:32.810
Amazingly, the guests did not balk.

01:31:32.810 --> 01:31:37.719
They loved not having to buy their coat back
from the coat check at the end of the meal

01:31:37.719 --> 01:31:40.159
because everything was included.

01:31:40.159 --> 01:31:44.990
And then every four or five months, we'd roll
it out at another restaurant.

01:31:44.990 --> 01:31:51.360
And we started to see our profits started
to erode a little bit.

01:31:51.360 --> 01:31:54.239
But damn it, I was gung-ho on making this
thing work.

01:31:54.239 --> 01:31:59.159
Because the good news was we were getting
better cooks and I knew we were doing the

01:31:59.159 --> 01:32:01.239
right thing.

01:32:01.239 --> 01:32:06.010
There's another thing that I should add is
that in making this choice, we actually had

01:32:06.010 --> 01:32:13.770
to forego a million dollars in federal tax
credits, like real money that they pay us.

01:32:13.770 --> 01:32:16.460
The federal government pays you to accept
tips.

01:32:16.460 --> 01:32:17.460
Tim Ferriss: What?

01:32:17.460 --> 01:32:18.460
Danny Meyer: I know.

01:32:18.460 --> 01:32:19.460
Does that seem crazy or what?

01:32:19.460 --> 01:32:20.460
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

01:32:20.460 --> 01:32:23.489
Danny Meyer: And once we eliminated tipping,
we had to forego those.

01:32:23.489 --> 01:32:28.880
And the reason they did that is that probably
15 years ago when the government realized

01:32:28.880 --> 01:32:34.750
they were not collecting a lot of taxes based
on tips because people were hiding what they

01:32:34.750 --> 01:32:41.600
were making, they actually brought the restaurant
industry under their cloak.

01:32:41.600 --> 01:32:48.070
And they said, "If you guys, for every dollar
tip you report to us that your servers are

01:32:48.070 --> 01:32:54.650
making, we will pay you back a certain percentage."

01:32:54.650 --> 01:33:00.090
And so that's why they pay restaurateurs to
take tips because they want the tips reported.

01:33:00.090 --> 01:33:02.800
So we gave up a million bucks in making this
choice.

01:33:02.800 --> 01:33:04.820
I just wanted to do the right thing.

01:33:04.820 --> 01:33:11.239
So now all of a sudden Covid happens, we're
basically brought to our knees with no revenues

01:33:11.239 --> 01:33:18.469
at all for four months, could have gone out
of business, and had to lay off a huge number

01:33:18.469 --> 01:33:20.040
of our team members.

01:33:20.040 --> 01:33:28.290
So finally in the summer of 2020, New York
made it legal to open your restaurant with

01:33:28.290 --> 01:33:30.600
a certain number of outdoor tables.

01:33:30.600 --> 01:33:35.590
You weren't going to make any money, but it
was going to helpfully get the city back up

01:33:35.590 --> 01:33:36.590
on its feet.

01:33:36.590 --> 01:33:41.760
And I was a big proponent for the city, for
our whole industry.

01:33:41.760 --> 01:33:45.820
And I saw this really amazing dynamic happen,
Tim.

01:33:45.820 --> 01:33:50.810
The New Yorkers who had been locked up in
their apartments for all these months, unable

01:33:50.810 --> 01:33:55.400
to go to restaurants, they could only get
curbside pickup and delivery and that kind

01:33:55.400 --> 01:34:00.700
of thing, were so incredibly grateful for
our servers who are willing to come out and

01:34:00.700 --> 01:34:07.600
serve them on the sidewalk that they were
literally throwing $20 bills at our servers,

01:34:07.600 --> 01:34:11.090
$50, $100 bills to say thank you.

01:34:11.090 --> 01:34:17.290
And now I'm telling our servers, "Not only
can you not accept that, but you have to tell

01:34:17.290 --> 01:34:20.900
our guests, 'You may not say thank you to
me.'"

01:34:20.900 --> 01:34:24.980
After about two weeks of this, I said — and
by the way, we had no vaccine at this point.

01:34:24.980 --> 01:34:30.580
So any server working there serving someone
who's not wearing a mask while they're eating,

01:34:30.580 --> 01:34:32.350
this was a dangerous time in New York.

01:34:32.350 --> 01:34:38.910
And I finally said, this is inhumane, this
is not being on our employees' side.

01:34:38.910 --> 01:34:40.780
And so I said, "Guess what?

01:34:40.780 --> 01:34:44.310
We are going to resume tipping.

01:34:44.310 --> 01:34:55.780
But in so doing, we as a company will start
to pay a percentage of our revenue every night

01:34:55.780 --> 01:34:59.239
to our cooks who are tip ineligible."

01:34:59.239 --> 01:35:03.659
I don't think that should be our responsibility,
but I did not want to erode the gains we had

01:35:03.659 --> 01:35:04.659
made.

01:35:04.659 --> 01:35:05.690
And so now that's where we are.

01:35:05.690 --> 01:35:11.810
So our servers are making tips, and if we
have a really busy night, our cooks are really

01:35:11.810 --> 01:35:12.810
happy, they're benefiting.

01:35:12.810 --> 01:35:18.010
They're not just, like in so many restaurants
where on a busy night you're watching the

01:35:18.010 --> 01:35:22.730
waiters counting their tips, and the cooks
just perspired more.

01:35:22.730 --> 01:35:23.929
That's not right.

01:35:23.929 --> 01:35:26.300
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:35:26.300 --> 01:35:30.530
I grew up earning my keep on Long Island as
a kid.

01:35:30.530 --> 01:35:31.949
I probably started really young.

01:35:31.949 --> 01:35:40.780
God, I was, don't know, 14 or 15, working
as a busboy, and occasionally, if the moment

01:35:40.780 --> 01:35:45.449
presented itself, a server at restaurants
on Eastern Long Island.

01:35:45.449 --> 01:35:49.610
So I grew up as a townie in the Hamptons,
which has this whole set of stories for another

01:35:49.610 --> 01:35:50.800
time.

01:35:50.800 --> 01:35:57.980
But I'm wondering, as we think about the four
quadrants that you described earlier, the

01:35:57.980 --> 01:36:04.380
can and will, the can't and won't, and the
other permutations, did you run into or how

01:36:04.380 --> 01:36:11.350
did you handle a dynamic where people who,
in the previous paradigm, so now I'm talking

01:36:11.350 --> 01:36:18.159
about in the Hospitality Included transition,
you're already there, but people who in the

01:36:18.159 --> 01:36:26.270
tipping environment had been top performers
contending with that?

01:36:26.270 --> 01:36:29.409
So the sunflowers that you want to water.

01:36:29.409 --> 01:36:36.530
How did you contend with, if you did, the
dynamic of maybe prior top performers feeling

01:36:36.530 --> 01:36:39.700
like they were not going to earn their fair
share?

01:36:39.700 --> 01:36:42.119
Danny Meyer: Yeah, it was really tough.

01:36:42.119 --> 01:36:48.670
And if you want to take a communications lesson,
just try rolling out Hospitality Included

01:36:48.670 --> 01:36:49.750
at your restaurants.

01:36:49.750 --> 01:36:57.670
Because tipping is such a deeply held American
way of life, both for the people providing

01:36:57.670 --> 01:37:00.409
the service and for the people on the receiving
end.

01:37:00.409 --> 01:37:05.030
This was a really tough thing to break, and
not many restaurants joined us in this.

01:37:05.030 --> 01:37:11.160
A couple tried and a lot of them folded their
hand well before we did on the whole thing.

01:37:11.160 --> 01:37:16.300
But what we did do, and I feel really good
about this, was to do the same thing we do

01:37:16.300 --> 01:37:17.330
with our cooks.

01:37:17.330 --> 01:37:20.760
So you get a raise based on merit if you're
a cook.

01:37:20.760 --> 01:37:27.090
If you're a server, you're getting the same
adjusted minimum wage no matter what you do.

01:37:27.090 --> 01:37:36.099
And so we created learning opportunities with
our teams and we had had several tiers of

01:37:36.099 --> 01:37:37.180
learning opportunities.

01:37:37.180 --> 01:37:42.360
And if you got to a different tier, you got
a higher base wage.

01:37:42.360 --> 01:37:47.280
Obviously we were not doing tipping, so you
could actually get a raise through the whole

01:37:47.280 --> 01:37:48.280
thing.

01:37:48.280 --> 01:37:53.590
So if you looked at our staff, we factored
in longevity, but sometimes the longest serving

01:37:53.590 --> 01:37:59.469
servers are the ones that most need to go
because they're just over ripe and they've

01:37:59.469 --> 01:38:00.949
lost their spunk.

01:38:00.949 --> 01:38:05.969
But we definitely factored in longevity because
here's the thing, the way you make the most

01:38:05.969 --> 01:38:10.099
tips in a restaurant is generally through
longevity, because you get classically, the

01:38:10.099 --> 01:38:16.630
Thursday, Friday, Saturday shifts, and classically
you don't get the Monday, Tuesday lunch kind

01:38:16.630 --> 01:38:18.219
of thing.

01:38:18.219 --> 01:38:26.390
What was great, and this was a win for everybody,
was that that while longevity would factor

01:38:26.390 --> 01:38:31.600
into your base rate, it did not factor into
your schedule.

01:38:31.600 --> 01:38:36.281
And so a lot of people who, in order to make
their money, had to work weekends, sometimes

01:38:36.281 --> 01:38:42.199
away from their family, they could actually
have a schedule that was a much better balance

01:38:42.199 --> 01:38:43.199
of life.

01:38:43.199 --> 01:38:45.000
So there was some wins in this.

01:38:45.000 --> 01:38:51.130
But at the end of the day, we just couldn't
make the math work and it just didn't seem

01:38:51.130 --> 01:38:54.619
right to tell our staff, "Stop.

01:38:54.619 --> 01:38:57.889
You must tell our guests, 'No thank you.'"

01:38:57.889 --> 01:38:59.311
That was a pretty awkward thing.

01:38:59.311 --> 01:39:05.780
And going back to Japan for a minute, they
have a wonderful culture, very different from

01:39:05.780 --> 01:39:12.239
ours, and they call it [foreign language],
which is their word, which is so much more

01:39:12.239 --> 01:39:14.070
than hospitality.

01:39:14.070 --> 01:39:25.110
It's the providing of hospitality and service
without expectation of any further reward,

01:39:25.110 --> 01:39:28.640
in anticipation of someone else's needs.

01:39:28.640 --> 01:39:34.610
And it's a beautiful concept, and that's how
that culture has been brought up.

01:39:34.610 --> 01:39:38.580
And I've learned a lot from it.

01:39:38.580 --> 01:39:44.210
I do believe that the kind of people we hire,
who we call 51 percenters, people who have

01:39:44.210 --> 01:39:50.510
a high hospitality quotient, genuinely are
happier themselves when they do something

01:39:50.510 --> 01:39:55.920
that makes you feel good, but that's no reason
that they should be penalized relative to

01:39:55.920 --> 01:39:59.520
this marketplace in terms of how much money
they can make.

01:39:59.520 --> 01:40:03.130
Tim Ferriss: What does 51 percent refer to?

01:40:03.130 --> 01:40:04.130
Why 51?

01:40:04.130 --> 01:40:07.600
Danny Meyer: Well, we want a hundred percent
employee, just like all of our employees would

01:40:07.600 --> 01:40:08.610
like us.

01:40:08.610 --> 01:40:11.150
I want to get a hundred on my test.

01:40:11.150 --> 01:40:14.730
I think that happened probably three times
when I was a kid, but it doesn't mean I didn't

01:40:14.730 --> 01:40:16.199
want it.

01:40:16.199 --> 01:40:20.220
And so it's our way of basically saying, "Great,
you want a hundred on your test, here's how

01:40:20.220 --> 01:40:21.250
you get it."

01:40:21.250 --> 01:40:26.770
Cool thing is there's only two ingredients
in this recipe.

01:40:26.770 --> 01:40:31.520
There's your technical performance, how well
you do the job you're paid to do.

01:40:31.520 --> 01:40:35.849
The most points you can get for doing it perfectly
is 49.

01:40:35.849 --> 01:40:38.900
Then there's your hospitality performance.

01:40:38.900 --> 01:40:42.790
That's how did you make everyone else feel
while you were doing it?

01:40:42.790 --> 01:40:44.720
That's worth 51 points.

01:40:44.720 --> 01:40:48.199
I think we both know 51 is a little more than
49.

01:40:48.199 --> 01:40:50.780
I don't want to get a 51 on my test.

01:40:50.780 --> 01:40:51.780
That's a failing grade.

01:40:51.780 --> 01:40:56.219
If I don't do the technical stuff right, the
food sucks or it took too long, doesn't matter

01:40:56.219 --> 01:40:58.310
how nice I am.

01:40:58.310 --> 01:40:59.780
I want a hundred.

01:40:59.780 --> 01:41:04.830
But I will tell you right now that the only
way I've learned to become essential and burrow

01:41:04.830 --> 01:41:11.670
our way into people's hearts is the food better
be damn good.

01:41:11.670 --> 01:41:14.530
But more than anything else, you've got to
feel like we're on your side.

01:41:14.530 --> 01:41:16.140
And that's where the 51 percent comes in.

01:41:16.140 --> 01:41:18.490
Tim Ferriss: Love that.

01:41:18.490 --> 01:41:23.679
Danny, I'd love to ask you just a few more
questions, and one that comes to mind, you

01:41:23.679 --> 01:41:29.050
seem to be, I have to imagine a reader.

01:41:29.050 --> 01:41:36.570
And besides your own books, what books have
you gifted the most to other people, if any

01:41:36.570 --> 01:41:38.610
come to mind, or gifted frequently?

01:41:38.610 --> 01:41:43.630
Danny Meyer: Well, outside of our cookbooks
and Setting the Table, which is the most gift

01:41:43.630 --> 01:41:48.889
I give because someone's got to give it, I
love marketing.

01:41:48.889 --> 01:41:54.840
I just love marketing because marketing is
understanding the other person.

01:41:54.840 --> 01:41:55.840
Marketing is a dialogue.

01:41:55.840 --> 01:41:58.300
It's a lot like hospitality.

01:41:58.300 --> 01:42:00.310
And I love Seth Godin's books.

01:42:00.310 --> 01:42:05.510
And I would say that I've given many of his
books, but probably the one that I've given

01:42:05.510 --> 01:42:07.280
the most is This Is Marketing.

01:42:07.280 --> 01:42:15.020
And it comes down to one simple express —
He's just so brilliant at getting to the essence

01:42:15.020 --> 01:42:17.510
of what something really is.

01:42:17.510 --> 01:42:26.250
And he thinks marketing is when you can convey
that people like us do things like this.

01:42:26.250 --> 01:42:29.160
And that sounds so simple, and yet it's so
deep actually.

01:42:29.160 --> 01:42:32.739
He understands that people want to feel — I
believe that the biggest longing people have

01:42:32.739 --> 01:42:34.590
is to belong.

01:42:34.590 --> 01:42:38.679
And so great marketing actually doesn't just
sell you something.

01:42:38.679 --> 01:42:42.030
It makes you feel like you belong to a tribe.

01:42:42.030 --> 01:42:47.920
So anyway, long answer to your question, but
that would be the one.

01:42:47.920 --> 01:42:50.320
Tim Ferriss: Seth also walks the walk.

01:42:50.320 --> 01:42:56.900
I really am a huge fan of Seth, and I've gotten
to know him over the years.

01:42:56.900 --> 01:43:01.150
Very sweet guy, always will tell you exactly
what is on his mind.

01:43:01.150 --> 01:43:03.929
You don't have to guess, which I love.

01:43:03.929 --> 01:43:08.650
Makes me pine after the East Coast every once
in a while when I have to deal with the opposite

01:43:08.650 --> 01:43:10.239
somewhere in the US.

01:43:10.239 --> 01:43:14.780
Danny Meyer: I hope Seth won't mind this story,
but he once told me exactly what was on his

01:43:14.780 --> 01:43:15.780
mind.

01:43:15.780 --> 01:43:23.800
He had a pretty bad service experience at
one of our restaurants, Maialino, and he told

01:43:23.800 --> 01:43:25.380
me.

01:43:25.380 --> 01:43:30.820
And I've known Seth for a while, love him.

01:43:30.820 --> 01:43:35.830
And I felt so badly, and I said, "I've got
to write a great next chapter on this.

01:43:35.830 --> 01:43:37.170
I've got to figure out something."

01:43:37.170 --> 01:43:43.080
So I invited Seth to have breakfast with me
at Maialino.

01:43:43.080 --> 01:43:45.739
And he didn't want to talk about the service
mishap.

01:43:45.739 --> 01:43:48.920
He said, "I gave you the gift, you go deal
with it.

01:43:48.920 --> 01:43:49.920
That's your problem.

01:43:49.920 --> 01:43:50.920
Go figure that out.

01:43:50.920 --> 01:43:53.440
I just want to have a good conversation with
you."

01:43:53.440 --> 01:43:55.110
So we had a great breakfast.

01:43:55.110 --> 01:43:59.990
And I'm going, all right, finally got Seth
back in my good graces here.

01:43:59.990 --> 01:44:06.110
As he's leaving the restaurant after breakfast,
I don't know what happened, but he banged

01:44:06.110 --> 01:44:08.290
into our clear door.

01:44:08.290 --> 01:44:12.510
The door was so clean that day, he banged
into it and broke his nose.

01:44:12.510 --> 01:44:18.280
And I'm going, now what am I going to do here?

01:44:18.280 --> 01:44:19.770
Oh, poor Seth.

01:44:19.770 --> 01:44:23.650
Anyway, he's a genius and he is the definition
of a mensch.

01:44:23.650 --> 01:44:25.670
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, he really is.

01:44:25.670 --> 01:44:34.560
And he's one of the best people I've ever
met at walking the walk with what he describes

01:44:34.560 --> 01:44:38.560
or his values, whether publicly presented
or not.

01:44:38.560 --> 01:44:44.560
He's very good at defining his values.

01:44:44.560 --> 01:44:48.010
And I don't think he would think of it this
maybe explicitly, but sort of rank ordering

01:44:48.010 --> 01:44:53.560
them and then organizing his life, making
decisions about family, business, et cetera,

01:44:53.560 --> 01:44:56.719
travel, that are aligned with all of those.

01:44:56.719 --> 01:44:58.139
It's very impressive.

01:44:58.139 --> 01:45:02.420
I aspire to be better at it, certainly.

01:45:02.420 --> 01:45:10.179
Leading off of the very pithy and very, I
think accurate also expression regarding marketing

01:45:10.179 --> 01:45:14.450
from Seth, if you could put anything on a
billboard, this is metaphorically speaking,

01:45:14.450 --> 01:45:22.119
just to get a message out, could be an image,
could be a quote, could be a word, could be

01:45:22.119 --> 01:45:27.260
anything at all, just to convey something
to a very, very large number of people, is

01:45:27.260 --> 01:45:30.159
there anything that you might put on that
billboard?

01:45:30.159 --> 01:45:38.219
Danny Meyer: Can't we please have a charitable
assumption about one another?

01:45:38.219 --> 01:45:40.220
I think I'd put that on there.

01:45:40.220 --> 01:45:48.099
I feel like when we go into a conversation
with somebody or read about somebody and we

01:45:48.099 --> 01:45:55.010
assume the worst as the starting point, it
doesn't usually end up very well.

01:45:55.010 --> 01:46:01.170
On the other hand, when you assume the best
intentions, you just never know.

01:46:01.170 --> 01:46:09.940
It's very possible that you hadn't communicated,
understood what someone really meant.

01:46:09.940 --> 01:46:18.389
And I feel like going into any relationship
or experience with a charitable assumption

01:46:18.389 --> 01:46:19.960
is such a helpful thing.

01:46:19.960 --> 01:46:23.330
Tim Ferriss: Is that something that you've
had out of the box?

01:46:23.330 --> 01:46:28.090
You mentioned the glass half full even before
you uncorked the bottle.

01:46:28.090 --> 01:46:35.360
But in this particular context, going into
say, conversations, having charitable assumptions

01:46:35.360 --> 01:46:37.980
as opposed to assuming the worst, is that
something you've cultivated?

01:46:37.980 --> 01:46:41.739
Is that something that just seems to come
with your hardwiring?

01:46:41.739 --> 01:46:44.090
Danny Meyer: I just think that's who I am.

01:46:44.090 --> 01:46:45.570
Tim Ferriss: Oh, great.

01:46:45.570 --> 01:46:50.719
Danny Meyer: I don't think it's something
I've ever thought consciously about, but I

01:46:50.719 --> 01:46:57.940
do know that it's not just about being optimistic,
but it's about being hopeful.

01:46:57.940 --> 01:46:59.800
There is a difference between hope and optimism.

01:46:59.800 --> 01:47:06.480
I think that hope is an active act.

01:47:06.480 --> 01:47:11.139
I think it's — so much of this gets back
to growing up.

01:47:11.139 --> 01:47:18.310
My parents got divorced after 25 years, and
I'm watching all these conversations and there's

01:47:18.310 --> 01:47:24.000
two truths to every single conversation.

01:47:24.000 --> 01:47:30.080
And I think one of the gifts I got as a kid
was getting to cherry-pick the good stuff

01:47:30.080 --> 01:47:33.449
from each of my parents and leave the bad.

01:47:33.449 --> 01:47:35.150
Didn't make them bad people.

01:47:35.150 --> 01:47:39.730
It's just like you pick and choose, but you
start from a standpoint, this person actually

01:47:39.730 --> 01:47:44.469
means well, their intention is to do the right
thing.

01:47:44.469 --> 01:47:50.300
And we're all flawed, we all make mistakes
constantly, but that's not a reason to vilify

01:47:50.300 --> 01:47:53.599
us.

01:47:53.599 --> 01:48:01.040
The other thing I'll just say is that you've
clearly gotten the sense I live my life within

01:48:01.040 --> 01:48:05.010
the 40-yard lines.

01:48:05.010 --> 01:48:09.489
I don't live my life on the five yard lines
where you can't hear people yelling at each

01:48:09.489 --> 01:48:13.670
other from the five yard lines of a football
field.

01:48:13.670 --> 01:48:18.820
And I know that we've gotten so tribal in
this country, so tribal with everything that

01:48:18.820 --> 01:48:22.530
if you're not all the way on one side or the
other, you're wrong.

01:48:22.530 --> 01:48:25.130
And that's just not how I look at life.

01:48:25.130 --> 01:48:30.889
It just gives me great joy to find how can
we make progress together?

01:48:30.889 --> 01:48:34.540
And it starts with assuming the best in people.

01:48:34.540 --> 01:48:40.530
Tim Ferriss: Danny, we've covered a lot of
ground and we're honing in on nearly two hours

01:48:40.530 --> 01:48:43.650
now or roughly two hours.

01:48:43.650 --> 01:48:52.260
And as we wind to a close, is there anything
else that you would like to say or request?

01:48:52.260 --> 01:48:57.600
Could be a request of my audience, could be
anything at all that maybe we didn't discuss

01:48:57.600 --> 01:48:58.670
that you'd like to bring up.

01:48:58.670 --> 01:49:03.760
Any closing comments, complaints you'd like
to lodge publicly?

01:49:03.760 --> 01:49:04.760
Anything at all?

01:49:04.760 --> 01:49:12.960
Danny Meyer: Go support your local restaurants
and your local butchers and your local fruit

01:49:12.960 --> 01:49:14.500
growers, et cetera.

01:49:14.500 --> 01:49:25.280
You cannot know how important for the economy
and also for just the emotional fabric of

01:49:25.280 --> 01:49:27.570
a community restaurants are.

01:49:27.570 --> 01:49:37.679
Restaurants provide a place for the community
to come together and do their social life,

01:49:37.679 --> 01:49:44.219
to do their business life, to do their personal
life.

01:49:44.219 --> 01:49:50.780
We saw what life looked like when we didn't
have restaurants during Covid, and it gets

01:49:50.780 --> 01:49:54.000
to the human desire to connect with people.

01:49:54.000 --> 01:49:55.920
People are so eager to be with people.

01:49:55.920 --> 01:50:03.280
And so the more you can do to support people
who work in the food industry, that's my hope.

01:50:03.280 --> 01:50:11.330
Tim Ferriss: I think that's a perfect place
to — and Danny, thank you so much for making

01:50:11.330 --> 01:50:12.330
the time.

01:50:12.330 --> 01:50:17.840
It's really nice to have a long-form conversation
with you.

01:50:17.840 --> 01:50:21.500
And I appreciate you carving time out of your
schedule to do this.

01:50:21.500 --> 01:50:23.719
So first and foremost, thank you very much.

01:50:23.719 --> 01:50:27.540
I have tons of notes, many things to follow
up on, many things to think about, which is

01:50:27.540 --> 01:50:29.230
always the sign.

01:50:29.230 --> 01:50:35.880
Danny Meyer: I'm really grateful to you and
thank you for sharing me with your amazing

01:50:35.880 --> 01:50:37.010
audience.

01:50:37.010 --> 01:50:39.389
And I hope I'll get to see you in New York.

01:50:39.389 --> 01:50:41.020
Tim Ferriss: Absolutely.

01:50:41.020 --> 01:50:42.560
I do love New York City.

01:50:42.560 --> 01:50:44.140
I mean, there's no place like New York City.

01:50:44.140 --> 01:50:46.010
Danny Meyer: Or at a Shake Shack near you.

01:50:46.010 --> 01:50:52.130
By the way, at the Shake Shack in Austin,
we have a burger there that we only do in

01:50:52.130 --> 01:50:53.130
Austin.

01:50:53.130 --> 01:50:54.130
Tim Ferriss: No kidding.

01:50:54.130 --> 01:50:56.230
All right, so I can find that on Lamar, on
the South Lamar location?

01:50:56.230 --> 01:50:57.230
Danny Meyer: You can find it there.

01:50:57.230 --> 01:50:59.659
And it's called the Lockhart Link.

01:50:59.659 --> 01:51:06.849
So we get an amazing sausage from Lockhart,
Texas and put it right on top of a Shack Burger.

01:51:06.849 --> 01:51:09.410
I wish we had those in New York, but only
in Austin.

01:51:09.410 --> 01:51:11.520
Tim Ferriss: All right, that's on my to-do
list.

01:51:11.520 --> 01:51:14.849
And for those who don't know, Lockhart, very
famous.

01:51:14.849 --> 01:51:22.690
This is one of the meccas of delicious meat
here in central Texas.

01:51:22.690 --> 01:51:25.300
So I will have to try the Lockhart Link.

01:51:25.300 --> 01:51:31.409
And for people listening, they can find you
Danny on Twitter @DHMeyer.

01:51:31.409 --> 01:51:32.690
Instagram also @DHMeyer.

01:51:32.690 --> 01:51:36.320
We'll link to many, many other things that
we've discussed.

01:51:36.320 --> 01:51:39.389
I recommend people check out Setting the Table,
your book.

01:51:39.389 --> 01:51:47.320
They can find USHG at ushg.com, as well as
your team member description and so on.

01:51:47.320 --> 01:51:51.989
And I will add links to everything we discussed
in the show notes for folks after the fact.

01:51:51.989 --> 01:51:56.440
And you'll be able to find that as always
at tim.blog/podcast.

01:51:56.440 --> 01:52:02.219
And until next time, be just a bit kinder
than you think is necessary to others and

01:52:02.219 --> 01:52:03.810
to yourself.

01:52:03.810 --> 01:52:07.840
Assume good intentions, make charitable assumptions
about the next person you're going to have

01:52:07.840 --> 01:52:09.320
a conversation with.

01:52:09.320 --> 01:52:11.389
And as always, thanks for tuning in.

